WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.134 --> 00:00:02.068
- Hey everybody welcome
to the podcast.

00:00:02.068 --> 00:00:04.504
My guest today is Karamo Brown.

00:00:04.504 --> 00:00:07.173
You know Karamo as
the culture expert

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:10.076
on the hit Netflix
show Queer Eye.

00:00:10.076 --> 00:00:14.180
What a beautiful, delightful
man and soul he is.

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:16.850
So hit that subscribe button,
and without further ado,

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.885
please enjoy our conversation.

00:00:18.885 --> 00:00:21.454
(upbeat music)

00:00:25.992 --> 00:00:27.227
Well, welcome to my home.

00:00:27.227 --> 00:00:28.995
I'm so delighted
to have you here.

00:00:28.995 --> 00:00:31.264
It's been a little bit in
the making to get you here,

00:00:31.264 --> 00:00:33.867
but I'm so excited, everybody.

00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:35.969
I mean, I've been doing
this for eight years,

00:00:35.969 --> 00:00:37.737
I've never seen my
family more excited

00:00:37.737 --> 00:00:40.340
to meet (indistinct).
(laughing)

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:43.109
They all turnout
here to welcome you.

00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:44.644
- I appreciate that.

00:00:44.644 --> 00:00:46.146
- And you asked, you asked
a minute ago, like why?

00:00:46.146 --> 00:00:48.014
I thought we were going to
do that in the black box room

00:00:48.014 --> 00:00:49.516
where we have been recording.

00:00:49.516 --> 00:00:51.951
Historically, we've always
done the podcast here.

00:00:51.951 --> 00:00:54.721
We have another room on the
other side, but when COVID hit

00:00:54.721 --> 00:00:58.425
we moved it out to a neutral
space just for safety reasons.

00:00:58.425 --> 00:01:00.527
But with you, it's like

00:01:00.527 --> 00:01:03.763
I can't record a
podcast with Karamo,

00:01:03.763 --> 00:01:04.898
like he has to come.

00:01:04.898 --> 00:01:07.267
So, that's the whole thing.

00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:08.902
This is what you do.
- I appreciate it.

00:01:08.902 --> 00:01:09.736
Your house gorgeous.

00:01:09.736 --> 00:01:10.970
- I appreciate that.

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:12.472
- And we find out
we're neighbors.

00:01:12.472 --> 00:01:14.574
- Yeah, I can't believe
that, that's a mindblower

00:01:14.574 --> 00:01:17.610
and we've got the doors open
and I've never sat so far away

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:18.845
from a podcast before.

00:01:18.845 --> 00:01:21.548
So we've undertaken
safety protocols

00:01:21.548 --> 00:01:22.982
to make sure we're safe.

00:01:22.982 --> 00:01:25.452
I know you've been tested
a lot as have I so,

00:01:25.452 --> 00:01:29.656
we can kind of breathe,
relax, and enjoy,

00:01:29.656 --> 00:01:31.124
each other's company man.

00:01:31.124 --> 00:01:34.027
You reached out to
me, which blew my mind

00:01:34.027 --> 00:01:37.864
because I had mentioned in
a podcast with my buddy Adam

00:01:37.864 --> 00:01:41.301
that our whole family had
been enjoying Queer Eye

00:01:41.301 --> 00:01:42.569
and I was late to the party.

00:01:42.569 --> 00:01:45.538
Like my relationship
with Queer Eye was

00:01:45.538 --> 00:01:48.274
the original cast in series.

00:01:48.274 --> 00:01:51.411
So, the new one
launched in 2018, right?

00:01:51.411 --> 00:01:52.245
- Yeah.

00:01:52.245 --> 00:01:53.713
- Spent five seasons.

00:01:53.713 --> 00:01:55.748
- The joke is whenever there's
a Republican in office,

00:01:55.748 --> 00:01:58.084
Queer Eye needs
to come back, so.

00:01:58.084 --> 00:01:59.052
- Ratings go up.

00:02:01.488 --> 00:02:03.923
And I had never watched,

00:02:03.923 --> 00:02:06.126
your version, the new
iteration of the show

00:02:06.126 --> 00:02:08.194
until the beginning
of the pandemic.

00:02:08.194 --> 00:02:11.698
And we just latched
onto it as a family.

00:02:11.698 --> 00:02:13.867
And it's like the one show
that we can all enjoy.

00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:15.201
Like if you met my whole family,

00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:17.403
lots of ages and you
know, going on here,

00:02:17.403 --> 00:02:19.906
and we would just look
forward to it every night.

00:02:19.906 --> 00:02:21.674
We went through all
five seasons and

00:02:21.674 --> 00:02:24.210
it was just
absolutely delightful.

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:25.378
And I was like, I can't believe,

00:02:25.378 --> 00:02:26.679
I didn't know about this show.

00:02:26.679 --> 00:02:28.982
And I was raving about
it on the podcast.

00:02:28.982 --> 00:02:30.550
And I couldn't believe it
when you reached out to me

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:33.153
because of course I'd
wanna talk to you.

00:02:33.153 --> 00:02:34.420
You're my guy.

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.256
- Well the love is
mutual obviously.

00:02:36.256 --> 00:02:37.190
Me reaching out to you was

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:39.325
cause I was like super fan.

00:02:39.325 --> 00:02:41.227
I just love the way
you have conversations,

00:02:41.227 --> 00:02:43.463
the way you engage your guests.

00:02:43.463 --> 00:02:46.733
And you know, I always,
even though I'm on this show

00:02:46.733 --> 00:02:48.501
I don't have this
Hollywood mentality yet.

00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:50.837
And I hope to never get
that Hollywood mentality.

00:02:50.837 --> 00:02:53.206
So I'm always like,
hey, you really cool.

00:02:53.206 --> 00:02:54.474
Do you think I'm cool too?

00:02:54.474 --> 00:02:57.043
I feel like a seventh
grader all the time.

00:02:57.043 --> 00:02:58.845
- I do too, it's funny.

00:02:58.845 --> 00:03:02.182
And I'll never forget Jaya,
our youngest who you met,

00:03:02.182 --> 00:03:04.450
who's a huge fan
of you in the show.

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.653
I was like Jaya,
Karamo DMed to me.

00:03:06.653 --> 00:03:07.820
He wants to come on the show

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:09.422
and just looked at me confused.

00:03:09.422 --> 00:03:11.891
She's like, what could
he possibly wanna talk

00:03:11.891 --> 00:03:12.725
to you about?

00:03:12.725 --> 00:03:14.861
(Karamo laughs)

00:03:14.861 --> 00:03:16.296
- Jaya, your dad is cool.

00:03:16.296 --> 00:03:17.530
Your dad is really cool.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:19.999
- Yeah, you're doing
me a solid in the eyes

00:03:19.999 --> 00:03:21.501
of my daughter by being here.

00:03:21.501 --> 00:03:24.103
- Awesome, is the one
that helped direct me?

00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:25.638
- No, that was Mathis.

00:03:25.638 --> 00:03:28.441
Jaya is the one with the
reddish (indistinct),

00:03:28.441 --> 00:03:29.576
yeah, she's 13.

00:03:31.044 --> 00:03:33.379
Anyway, happy to be
here today with you.

00:03:33.379 --> 00:03:34.380
- [Karamo] Same.

00:03:35.582 --> 00:03:38.451
- And you know, I wanna
kind of unpack the show

00:03:38.451 --> 00:03:40.486
and your personal story,

00:03:40.486 --> 00:03:44.857
but in thinking about how
to approach this with you,

00:03:44.857 --> 00:03:49.662
I was thinking about like what
makes Queer Eyes so magical?

00:03:49.662 --> 00:03:52.131
And I think what it
is in my mind at least

00:03:52.131 --> 00:03:54.400
and I'm interested
in what you think,

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:57.971
to me it's like this
antidote to our,

00:03:57.971 --> 00:04:01.774
very kind of acrimonious
divided culture at the moment

00:04:01.774 --> 00:04:05.378
because it's so infused with
hope and love and compassion

00:04:05.378 --> 00:04:08.982
and understanding, it's
devoid of all the judgment.

00:04:08.982 --> 00:04:11.584
That seems to be the
touchstone of all other forms

00:04:11.584 --> 00:04:14.787
of media that are popular or
that we're consuming right now.

00:04:14.787 --> 00:04:18.691
And I can only presume that
that was very intentional

00:04:18.691 --> 00:04:19.692
from the get-go.

00:04:19.692 --> 00:04:20.760
- Yeah, it was.

00:04:21.961 --> 00:04:25.198
I have told this
once before that,

00:04:25.198 --> 00:04:28.968
one of the reasons I believe
that the five of us got the job

00:04:28.968 --> 00:04:31.604
is because when they got
down to the final casting

00:04:31.604 --> 00:04:34.207
where they brought
like 60 to 70 guys

00:04:34.207 --> 00:04:37.443
into like do chemistry
tests, to see which five.

00:04:37.443 --> 00:04:38.745
So people were
different categories

00:04:38.745 --> 00:04:41.047
and they just start
putting you together.

00:04:41.047 --> 00:04:44.817
One of the things where
within the first hour,

00:04:44.817 --> 00:04:47.754
myself, Bobby and Tan connected

00:04:47.754 --> 00:04:50.356
then it was Antony within
an hour and a half.

00:04:50.356 --> 00:04:53.693
And then Jonathan within
two hours was because

00:04:53.693 --> 00:04:55.194
they would take you in this room

00:04:55.194 --> 00:04:56.829
and they would ask you questions

00:04:56.829 --> 00:04:59.565
and people will come out and
say, what did they ask you?

00:04:59.565 --> 00:05:02.735
And we were the only five
that were willing to tell

00:05:02.735 --> 00:05:04.437
what was being
asked in the room.

00:05:04.437 --> 00:05:07.407
Other people were,
you know, were like

00:05:07.407 --> 00:05:09.075
I'm not telling you,
you're my competition.

00:05:09.075 --> 00:05:10.810
But we just didn't feel like,

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:12.445
we were thinking to ourselves.

00:05:12.445 --> 00:05:14.414
Well, I was personally and
now talking to the guys

00:05:14.414 --> 00:05:16.115
and I still get these
little goosebumps.

00:05:16.115 --> 00:05:17.350
We were thinking,

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:20.353
well, if the show is
to make people better,

00:05:20.353 --> 00:05:22.021
the best people should get it

00:05:22.021 --> 00:05:23.956
but you should be wanting
to make people better.

00:05:23.956 --> 00:05:26.192
And if you can't
do it in a casting,

00:05:26.192 --> 00:05:29.162
then how are you authentically
gonna do it out here because

00:05:30.263 --> 00:05:32.098
like every round
someone would get cut

00:05:32.098 --> 00:05:33.633
and you never know
who's gonna be.

00:05:33.633 --> 00:05:36.035
So if I'm getting cut and I
could have maybe helped you

00:05:36.035 --> 00:05:39.505
a little bit along your
way, then you know, why not?

00:05:39.505 --> 00:05:42.342
And the casting director
said it was so apparent

00:05:42.342 --> 00:05:44.377
because they were
listening in on everything

00:05:44.377 --> 00:05:46.012
that we would come out
and we would be like,

00:05:46.012 --> 00:05:47.313
yeah, here's what happened.

00:05:47.313 --> 00:05:48.548
Or here's some advice for you.

00:05:48.548 --> 00:05:50.049
Here's how you could do it.

00:05:50.049 --> 00:05:53.353
And, I think that was part
of on top of the chemistry,

00:05:53.353 --> 00:05:56.155
on top of all of us being
great at what we do.

00:05:56.155 --> 00:05:57.924
I think it was them seeing like

00:05:57.924 --> 00:06:02.562
these people actually are living
this idea of not dividing,

00:06:02.562 --> 00:06:05.298
not judging, not hurting others.

00:06:05.298 --> 00:06:06.999
Like they really just wanna help

00:06:06.999 --> 00:06:08.534
cause they're doing
it in the casting.

00:06:08.534 --> 00:06:10.269
- The universe is abundant

00:06:10.269 --> 00:06:12.271
as opposed to a zero
sum game situation.

00:06:12.271 --> 00:06:13.773
- [Karamo] Completely.

00:06:13.773 --> 00:06:16.776
- And the casting was like a
year and a half for this show.

00:06:16.776 --> 00:06:19.712
Some crazy long period
of time, may seem--

00:06:19.712 --> 00:06:20.813
- Yeah for some,

00:06:20.813 --> 00:06:24.550
my casting was only two weeks.

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:25.985
So I had actually,

00:06:27.153 --> 00:06:28.755
Jonathan had been in
for a year and a half.

00:06:28.755 --> 00:06:31.290
I think Tan had been in
for a year and a half.

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:32.825
I came in two weeks before

00:06:32.825 --> 00:06:34.127
they were doing
that chemistry test.

00:06:34.127 --> 00:06:37.497
I found out about the
show through watching

00:06:37.497 --> 00:06:39.932
Watch What Happens
Live with Andy Cohen.

00:06:39.932 --> 00:06:42.402
Carson Kressley who was in
the original cast was on.

00:06:42.402 --> 00:06:44.837
And he was saying they
were rebooting the show.

00:06:44.837 --> 00:06:48.408
And I called my agent and my
agent got on this told me,

00:06:48.408 --> 00:06:50.476
no, you already have a job.

00:06:50.476 --> 00:06:52.211
Cause I was filming
a show for MTV,

00:06:52.211 --> 00:06:54.147
said we're not gonna
put you up for it.

00:06:54.147 --> 00:06:56.449
And I got off that
phone and I was like,

00:06:56.449 --> 00:06:57.850
I'm not accepting this now.

00:06:57.850 --> 00:07:00.286
This is not a no
that I'm okay with.

00:07:00.286 --> 00:07:02.755
And I called him
back and kept saying

00:07:02.755 --> 00:07:04.056
we need to keep working.

00:07:04.056 --> 00:07:05.057
We need to figure this out.

00:07:05.057 --> 00:07:06.592
And not in a, you know what,

00:07:06.592 --> 00:07:08.428
egotistical or mean way.

00:07:08.428 --> 00:07:10.463
It was just like sometimes
you have to follow

00:07:10.463 --> 00:07:11.931
and trust your instincts.

00:07:11.931 --> 00:07:13.866
And I think that
the universe will,

00:07:13.866 --> 00:07:15.334
or people in your circles

00:07:15.334 --> 00:07:17.970
will sometimes make you doubt
what you know to be true.

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:20.606
And I knew that, whether
I got the show or not

00:07:20.606 --> 00:07:22.909
I needed to be exposed
to this opportunity

00:07:22.909 --> 00:07:24.410
and to these people.

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:27.580
And I kept saying, you got
to, you got to, for a week.

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:32.285
And finally my agent found
someone who he knew in casting

00:07:32.285 --> 00:07:33.786
from a long time ago.

00:07:33.786 --> 00:07:36.689
And they were like, we'll
give him 10 minutes on,

00:07:36.689 --> 00:07:37.790
this is before Zoom,

00:07:37.790 --> 00:07:39.492
almost said Zoom, Skype.

00:07:39.492 --> 00:07:40.993
And were like, we can Skype him

00:07:40.993 --> 00:07:44.597
and see if he's even worthy
to come to the chemistry test.

00:07:44.597 --> 00:07:47.200
- Wow, cause it had already
been going on for so long,

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:48.901
you came late into the game.

00:07:48.901 --> 00:07:51.704
- Yeah, and they said
after the 10 minutes,

00:07:51.704 --> 00:07:53.105
they were like,
we'll bring you in.

00:07:53.105 --> 00:07:54.040
- [Rich] Wow.

00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:56.142
- And I asked, why was it?

00:07:56.142 --> 00:07:57.310
And they were like, well,

00:07:57.310 --> 00:08:00.112
you were the first
person who talked about

00:08:00.112 --> 00:08:02.882
the culture category from
a mental health standpoint.

00:08:02.882 --> 00:08:05.218
Everyone else was artists,

00:08:05.218 --> 00:08:08.821
musicians, you
know, playwrights.

00:08:08.821 --> 00:08:10.857
You're the first one that
said mental health need to be

00:08:10.857 --> 00:08:13.326
the component of the
show that was missing.

00:08:13.326 --> 00:08:15.194
And they were like, we
were interested and curious

00:08:15.194 --> 00:08:16.262
of what you wanna do with it.

00:08:16.262 --> 00:08:17.964
And obviously it worked.

00:08:17.964 --> 00:08:19.365
- Yeah, yeah.

00:08:19.365 --> 00:08:20.900
- It worked.

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:23.236
- That term culture though is
a legacy of the original show.

00:08:23.236 --> 00:08:24.704
And I think I said
in the other podcast,

00:08:24.704 --> 00:08:25.972
I was like, I'm confused.

00:08:25.972 --> 00:08:27.206
Like, cause it's not like,

00:08:27.206 --> 00:08:29.442
what you do is so much broader,

00:08:29.442 --> 00:08:32.745
culture just seems a misnomer
to characterize your function

00:08:32.745 --> 00:08:34.146
on the program.

00:08:34.146 --> 00:08:35.748
- Yeah, I agree with you.

00:08:35.748 --> 00:08:39.285
And it was a struggle for me
season one, because people,

00:08:39.285 --> 00:08:40.586
especially season one,
people would be like,

00:08:40.586 --> 00:08:42.188
we don't know what he does

00:08:42.188 --> 00:08:43.923
because the show is
being established.

00:08:43.923 --> 00:08:46.125
So it was like, he's
having long conversations.

00:08:46.125 --> 00:08:47.560
What is his thing?

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:49.428
Especially when you have people
who do physical attributes

00:08:49.428 --> 00:08:51.697
like you see a home,
you see someone's hair,

00:08:51.697 --> 00:08:54.400
you see what's on their
plate, you see their fashion,

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:55.801
you know having a conversation.

00:08:55.801 --> 00:08:57.770
People were like,
I don't get it.

00:08:58.838 --> 00:09:00.373
And when you don't have a title

00:09:00.373 --> 00:09:02.208
that clearly helps
people understand

00:09:02.208 --> 00:09:04.110
that he's working on the
mental and emotional state,

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:04.977
it was hard.

00:09:06.112 --> 00:09:08.080
But I had to release
my ego and say,

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:10.283
if people don't
get what I'm doing,

00:09:10.283 --> 00:09:12.151
as long as the
person we're helping,

00:09:12.151 --> 00:09:13.586
that's all that matters.

00:09:13.586 --> 00:09:15.254
And people finally
figured it out.

00:09:15.254 --> 00:09:17.390
They're like, oh, he's the guy.

00:09:17.390 --> 00:09:19.292
And I think it's
because of the crying,

00:09:19.292 --> 00:09:21.427
like every time I'd
get on a screen,

00:09:21.427 --> 00:09:22.728
the person will start crying.

00:09:22.728 --> 00:09:24.397
They're like, okay, we can,

00:09:24.397 --> 00:09:26.198
oh, we understand
what's going on here.

00:09:26.198 --> 00:09:27.466
Yeah, and so.

00:09:27.466 --> 00:09:29.635
- Well, I'm crying,
my family's crying.

00:09:29.635 --> 00:09:30.736
We're all crying.

00:09:30.736 --> 00:09:32.271
You know, I was like, oh my God,

00:09:32.271 --> 00:09:34.774
who knew this was gonna be
such an emotional journey

00:09:34.774 --> 00:09:35.908
week in, week out.

00:09:35.908 --> 00:09:37.543
Like you just crush it.

00:09:37.543 --> 00:09:41.180
Like there's no, like missteps.

00:09:41.180 --> 00:09:43.182
Was there ever an episode
where you spent a week

00:09:43.182 --> 00:09:46.052
with somebody and you're
like, this isn't working.

00:09:46.052 --> 00:09:47.920
- Yeah, twice.

00:09:49.155 --> 00:09:50.389
I always joke to people.

00:09:50.389 --> 00:09:51.924
If you go back and
watch the episodes,

00:09:51.924 --> 00:09:55.161
if Jonathan doesn't twirl
up the room, you can tell,

00:09:55.161 --> 00:09:57.663
cause he's probably the one
that's gonna always share

00:09:57.663 --> 00:09:58.798
through his body language

00:09:58.798 --> 00:10:00.533
what we're all
feeling collectively.

00:10:00.533 --> 00:10:02.068
If he doesn't twirl
a lot of room,

00:10:02.068 --> 00:10:03.869
it most likely means that
we were just ready to go.

00:10:03.869 --> 00:10:04.904
(both laugh)

00:10:04.904 --> 00:10:06.472
- So you've been
like two days in

00:10:06.472 --> 00:10:09.175
and just pull up the stakes
and like, we're done with this.

00:10:09.175 --> 00:10:10.643
It's not happening.

00:10:10.643 --> 00:10:12.378
- Yeah, there's two people
for sure that I know.

00:10:12.378 --> 00:10:15.147
And it pisses me off, if I
can be very honest with you.

00:10:15.147 --> 00:10:16.682
Like when I think back
about those people.

00:10:16.682 --> 00:10:19.385
So I keep up with all the
individuals we've ever helped,

00:10:19.385 --> 00:10:21.020
especially with my role of...

00:10:21.020 --> 00:10:22.655
- Yeah, that was a big
question that I had.

00:10:22.655 --> 00:10:24.523
- Yeah, I think it's important.

00:10:24.523 --> 00:10:26.392
I don't think it's
healthy to open people up

00:10:26.392 --> 00:10:29.028
and not give them an
opportunity to still do,

00:10:29.028 --> 00:10:30.262
to speak to you,

00:10:30.262 --> 00:10:31.931
to talk about whatever
you talk to them about,

00:10:31.931 --> 00:10:33.432
give them that
sort of aftercare.

00:10:33.432 --> 00:10:35.568
And the show does provide
aftercare as well.

00:10:35.568 --> 00:10:36.702
So, it's not all on me.

00:10:36.702 --> 00:10:37.970
- Oh, that's good to know.

00:10:37.970 --> 00:10:40.272
- Yeah, so we make sure
that they're good, but

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:43.376
there are some individuals that,

00:10:43.376 --> 00:10:46.245
the two that I'm thinking of
that made me so mad because

00:10:46.245 --> 00:10:48.047
we have so many
people who will apply

00:10:48.047 --> 00:10:49.682
and who would love
this opportunity.

00:10:49.682 --> 00:10:51.450
And when you get
someone who slipped

00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:53.653
through the cracks somehow,
and then comes here

00:10:53.653 --> 00:10:56.188
and doesn't take full advantage

00:10:56.188 --> 00:10:58.791
of five experts in their field,

00:10:58.791 --> 00:11:01.627
take advantage of a
crew of 70 people,

00:11:01.627 --> 00:11:04.230
take advantage of
all this exposure

00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:05.464
that you could get afterwards.

00:11:05.464 --> 00:11:08.134
And they're just
like, eh, I don't,

00:11:08.134 --> 00:11:10.269
it just upsets me.

00:11:10.269 --> 00:11:11.470
And like, you know,

00:11:11.470 --> 00:11:12.405
a few things really,
really upset me

00:11:12.405 --> 00:11:13.606
but that's one of the one.

00:11:13.606 --> 00:11:17.443
- Yeah, well, I mean,
they're not ready.

00:11:17.443 --> 00:11:19.111
I mean, that's a fear
response, isn't it?

00:11:19.111 --> 00:11:20.813
- Oh, completely
a fear response.

00:11:20.813 --> 00:11:23.115
But you know what the show is.

00:11:24.283 --> 00:11:25.518
- Who doesn't want
Bobby to come in

00:11:25.518 --> 00:11:27.386
and like completely
redo their house like

00:11:27.386 --> 00:11:28.320
by snapping his fingers.

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:29.255
- Well, that's the thing.

00:11:29.255 --> 00:11:30.489
And what we realized and,

00:11:30.489 --> 00:11:32.525
our casting department is great.

00:11:32.525 --> 00:11:34.393
They've started to
get savvy on that.

00:11:34.393 --> 00:11:37.963
Is it just because they want
the free house makeover.

00:11:37.963 --> 00:11:40.066
And I think that's the key
where these two individuals

00:11:40.066 --> 00:11:41.701
were like, yeah,
come and do my house.

00:11:41.701 --> 00:11:43.869
And they played the game
with the other categories.

00:11:43.869 --> 00:11:46.739
But you know, now we're,

00:11:46.739 --> 00:11:48.641
that's like the last thing

00:11:48.641 --> 00:11:51.544
that they find out
that they might get.

00:11:51.544 --> 00:11:54.914
- Well, all of you
guys could experts

00:11:54.914 --> 00:11:58.484
in your respective
fields, but the chemistry

00:11:58.484 --> 00:12:01.921
that you guys have together
is clearly the gold.

00:12:01.921 --> 00:12:05.658
I mean it's unbelievable the
way that you guys gel together.

00:12:05.658 --> 00:12:09.261
So taking it back to
that casting experience,

00:12:09.261 --> 00:12:10.496
was the casting director,

00:12:10.496 --> 00:12:12.298
like, okay, you go in
and you do your audition

00:12:12.298 --> 00:12:14.033
and then you come out and
then they're watching like

00:12:14.033 --> 00:12:15.868
how everybody's interacting
with each other,

00:12:15.868 --> 00:12:17.403
cause that's key.

00:12:17.403 --> 00:12:19.338
Like if you don't have that
the show is not gonna work.

00:12:19.338 --> 00:12:22.108
- Yeah, they were watching
both inside the room

00:12:22.108 --> 00:12:24.009
and outside of the room to see.

00:12:24.009 --> 00:12:26.879
And like I said, it took
us an hour and a half

00:12:26.879 --> 00:12:30.015
on the first day for the
five of us to come together.

00:12:30.015 --> 00:12:32.351
And the ironic part is,
the five of us didn't know

00:12:32.351 --> 00:12:33.619
if we were competing
against each other

00:12:33.619 --> 00:12:35.187
if we were in the
same category now.

00:12:35.187 --> 00:12:38.657
So we were hanging and
didn't know because it wasn't

00:12:38.657 --> 00:12:40.893
until the second day that
they gave everyone stickers,

00:12:40.893 --> 00:12:43.295
that said, like your
culture, your food.

00:12:43.295 --> 00:12:46.232
So I didn't know what
category anyone could be in.

00:12:46.232 --> 00:12:48.267
And they didn't know
what category I was in.

00:12:48.267 --> 00:12:49.668
And I think that helped

00:12:49.668 --> 00:12:51.771
that we organically
found each other.

00:12:53.072 --> 00:12:55.808
Awesomely we're not
in the same category.

00:12:56.675 --> 00:12:58.077
We were also diverse.

00:12:58.077 --> 00:12:59.612
And it was like, sort of like,

00:12:59.612 --> 00:13:02.047
I think things just
work out the way

00:13:02.047 --> 00:13:03.382
that they need to because.

00:13:03.382 --> 00:13:04.717
And then once we
got into the room,

00:13:04.717 --> 00:13:08.087
they would bring other
people in and you would,

00:13:08.087 --> 00:13:10.990
eventually it just was
the five of us in there.

00:13:10.990 --> 00:13:12.558
And then they had this other,

00:13:12.558 --> 00:13:15.361
another five five squad who was,

00:13:15.361 --> 00:13:17.062
I guess there are other choice

00:13:17.062 --> 00:13:18.964
which were all
phenomenal people.

00:13:18.964 --> 00:13:22.168
But I still remember on
that last night, I was like,

00:13:22.168 --> 00:13:23.702
they were like,

00:13:23.702 --> 00:13:24.970
well, we'll tell which one of
you groups are gonna make it.

00:13:24.970 --> 00:13:26.405
I looked at the other
group and I was like,

00:13:26.405 --> 00:13:28.307
you are great, but I'm sorry,
you're not getting this.

00:13:28.307 --> 00:13:30.376
I was like, sorry.

00:13:30.376 --> 00:13:32.611
You're not getting this at all.

00:13:32.611 --> 00:13:33.646
Yeah, yeah.

00:13:33.646 --> 00:13:35.681
I know, I still (indistinct).

00:13:36.916 --> 00:13:38.584
- It's gotta be a
little disorienting

00:13:38.584 --> 00:13:39.819
with the success
of the show though.

00:13:39.819 --> 00:13:43.055
I mean, you guys just
got Emmy nomination,

00:13:43.055 --> 00:13:45.357
it's like a cultural phenomenon.

00:13:45.357 --> 00:13:46.625
- Yeah, it's humbling.

00:13:46.625 --> 00:13:47.860
- But you've been
on TV for a while

00:13:47.860 --> 00:13:49.328
doing all kinds
of different stuff

00:13:49.328 --> 00:13:50.830
but nothing at this scale.

00:13:50.830 --> 00:13:52.064
- No, no, no, yeah.

00:13:52.064 --> 00:13:54.133
After college, I
did The Real World

00:13:54.133 --> 00:13:56.936
and then stopped doing
anything because,

00:13:56.936 --> 00:14:00.239
went on my journey of sobriety
and take care of my kids

00:14:00.239 --> 00:14:02.775
and then came back
out here to establish

00:14:02.775 --> 00:14:04.977
a real career in television.

00:14:04.977 --> 00:14:08.180
So for four years I was
doing like digital shows

00:14:08.180 --> 00:14:09.715
for the Oprah Winfrey network.

00:14:09.715 --> 00:14:11.417
I worked at HuffPost Live
and things of that nature.

00:14:11.417 --> 00:14:15.688
And so this was my
real first job ever,

00:14:16.589 --> 00:14:18.090
even though those were all jobs.

00:14:18.090 --> 00:14:21.527
And it is overwhelming and
it's humbling because again,

00:14:21.527 --> 00:14:23.729
I promise you if
you meet any of us,

00:14:23.729 --> 00:14:26.398
none of us have gotten
that celebrity bug.

00:14:26.398 --> 00:14:27.700
You know, like you meet
people in this town

00:14:27.700 --> 00:14:29.101
and you're just like,

00:14:29.101 --> 00:14:30.102
you're like, come on now.

00:14:30.102 --> 00:14:31.503
Like, you're still human being.

00:14:31.503 --> 00:14:32.805
I know how you
put on your pants.

00:14:32.805 --> 00:14:34.039
I know how you brush your teeth.

00:14:34.039 --> 00:14:37.509
Like the, well let's
treat each other as if

00:14:37.509 --> 00:14:38.777
we're all the same.

00:14:38.777 --> 00:14:40.212
Just because we have a
little bit more money

00:14:40.212 --> 00:14:41.647
or you have a little
bit more success,

00:14:41.647 --> 00:14:42.982
we have to remember those
things are fleeting.

00:14:42.982 --> 00:14:44.550
And the five of
us remember that.

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:48.120
And so, luckily we have each
other to ground each other

00:14:48.120 --> 00:14:49.355
which is nice.

00:14:49.355 --> 00:14:50.890
- That's beautiful.

00:14:50.890 --> 00:14:53.325
I mean, that's just walking
the talk of the show though.

00:14:53.325 --> 00:14:54.393
You know what I mean?

00:14:54.393 --> 00:14:55.728
- We try.

00:14:55.728 --> 00:14:59.965
- Yeah, well, you've
done the work.

00:14:59.965 --> 00:15:02.701
I mean your background
is pretty extraordinary

00:15:02.701 --> 00:15:05.237
and the obstacles that you've
had to face and overcome

00:15:05.237 --> 00:15:06.472
to get to this place.

00:15:06.472 --> 00:15:09.008
So let's like take it
back there for a minute.

00:15:09.008 --> 00:15:09.942
- Sure.

00:15:09.942 --> 00:15:11.410
- Growing up in the South.

00:15:11.410 --> 00:15:14.079
- Yeah, growing in the
South, immigrant parents.

00:15:14.079 --> 00:15:15.481
- Texas and Florida, right?

00:15:15.481 --> 00:15:17.182
- Texas and Florida, my
parents are Jamaican and Cuban.

00:15:17.182 --> 00:15:20.252
So, you know,

00:15:20.252 --> 00:15:25.257
being a little gay black
boy to immigrant parents,

00:15:26.458 --> 00:15:28.294
growing up in Texas and
Florida had its moments,

00:15:28.294 --> 00:15:29.929
let's say the least.

00:15:29.929 --> 00:15:34.934
You could feel, you could see,
the racism, the homophobia.

00:15:35.801 --> 00:15:37.269
And I was just the direct target

00:15:37.269 --> 00:15:39.204
because one of the things
that I always say to my father

00:15:40.639 --> 00:15:43.075
would always, he had this
map like old school map,

00:15:43.075 --> 00:15:45.077
like a MapQuest store ways.

00:15:45.077 --> 00:15:46.612
And he would go through it

00:15:46.612 --> 00:15:50.616
and he would basically red
line the school districts

00:15:50.616 --> 00:15:53.319
and see what apartment
complex we could live in

00:15:53.319 --> 00:15:57.089
so that we could go to the
school that was best funded.

00:15:57.089 --> 00:15:59.525
And unfortunately, the way
our system is worked out

00:15:59.525 --> 00:16:01.160
mostly the best
funded schools are

00:16:01.160 --> 00:16:02.761
in predominantly
white neighborhoods.

00:16:02.761 --> 00:16:05.564
And so I was always that
one apartment complex

00:16:05.564 --> 00:16:07.433
that was right on the outskirt

00:16:07.433 --> 00:16:10.502
of these 1 million, 2
million, $3 million homes.

00:16:10.502 --> 00:16:14.239
And then I walked into
the school and I'm one of

00:16:14.239 --> 00:16:18.377
three African-Americans or
one of five children of color.

00:16:18.377 --> 00:16:21.613
One of two kids who
had immigrant parents.

00:16:22.815 --> 00:16:26.185
I was the only one in
high school that had,

00:16:26.185 --> 00:16:30.689
decided to let people into
their life about my sexuality.

00:16:30.689 --> 00:16:35.427
So it caused this extreme
conflict consistently.

00:16:35.427 --> 00:16:38.597
It was like my existence
was always in conflict

00:16:38.597 --> 00:16:39.865
with where I was.

00:16:39.865 --> 00:16:42.468
And as a child, you
don't understand why

00:16:42.468 --> 00:16:43.769
your life is conflict

00:16:43.769 --> 00:16:46.572
and why you are the
reason for conflict.

00:16:46.572 --> 00:16:48.040
And as I got older,

00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:49.908
I started to realize that
this was the biggest blessing,

00:16:49.908 --> 00:16:52.244
all these different
identities and managing them

00:16:52.244 --> 00:16:54.880
because now I use
those conflicts

00:16:54.880 --> 00:16:57.516
as tools to help other
people navigate their own.

00:16:57.516 --> 00:17:00.386
I use those conflicts
to be more empathetic,

00:17:00.386 --> 00:17:02.654
to like understand where
people are coming from

00:17:02.654 --> 00:17:05.958
versus it could have made the
opposite of me being like...

00:17:05.958 --> 00:17:06.792
- Victim.

00:17:06.792 --> 00:17:08.494
- Yeah, F the world.

00:17:08.494 --> 00:17:10.629
- Well, your dad's
intention feels like

00:17:10.629 --> 00:17:11.930
it came from a good place.

00:17:11.930 --> 00:17:13.432
Like he's trying to
find the best school--

00:17:13.432 --> 00:17:15.434
- Of course, yeah, I know
that was a great place.

00:17:15.434 --> 00:17:16.969
- It's like a by-product of that

00:17:16.969 --> 00:17:19.038
is everything that you had
to go through and suffer.

00:17:19.038 --> 00:17:19.872
- Yeah, yeah.

00:17:19.872 --> 00:17:21.073
And, you know as dads,

00:17:21.073 --> 00:17:22.307
I appreciate it.

00:17:22.307 --> 00:17:24.176
So I don't talk about
that in a bad thing.

00:17:24.176 --> 00:17:25.644
The one thing I do talk about

00:17:25.644 --> 00:17:26.945
and the reason I bring that
story up specifically is

00:17:26.945 --> 00:17:28.480
because that's still
the problem today.

00:17:28.480 --> 00:17:31.483
We have an underfunded
education system

00:17:31.483 --> 00:17:33.085
and it only gets worse

00:17:33.085 --> 00:17:35.087
as you get into low
income neighborhoods.

00:17:35.087 --> 00:17:38.657
And I think sometimes we have
to really take a look and say

00:17:38.657 --> 00:17:40.692
what is wrong with
us as a society

00:17:40.692 --> 00:17:43.695
when kids are not our priority,
that's the biggest thing.

00:17:43.695 --> 00:17:45.330
Like when we saw with
the gun violence,

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:46.365
cause you know that
was my high school,

00:17:46.365 --> 00:17:48.133
Marjory Stoneman Douglas,

00:17:48.133 --> 00:17:50.903
where you know, the
high school students.

00:17:50.903 --> 00:17:52.337
Yeah, Parkland.

00:17:52.337 --> 00:17:56.141
I always think if
those kids dying

00:17:56.141 --> 00:17:58.877
can't get us to get some
comprehensive gun legislation.

00:17:58.877 --> 00:18:02.114
If kids not being able to
eat in school and passing out

00:18:02.114 --> 00:18:03.949
can't get us to
start feeding kids.

00:18:03.949 --> 00:18:06.185
If like, who are we as a society

00:18:06.185 --> 00:18:08.287
if we won't even take
care of our children?

00:18:08.287 --> 00:18:11.223
Like, and that's one of the
thing that blows my mind.

00:18:11.223 --> 00:18:13.525
And I'm sure as you, as
well as a parent, it's like

00:18:13.525 --> 00:18:16.428
there's no way one of your
kid's friends will come in here

00:18:16.428 --> 00:18:19.665
and say I'm hungry or I
don't have a book to read

00:18:19.665 --> 00:18:21.333
and you'd be like,
sorry, go home.

00:18:21.333 --> 00:18:22.701
You would feed them
and give them a book.

00:18:22.701 --> 00:18:25.170
And you know, it
just blows my mind.

00:18:25.170 --> 00:18:28.107
- Yeah, was your high school
the same high schools.

00:18:28.107 --> 00:18:30.209
I mean part, was it a
different high school?

00:18:30.209 --> 00:18:31.944
- No, the same high school,
Marjory Stoneman Douglas.

00:18:31.944 --> 00:18:32.978
Yeah, that was my high school.

00:18:32.978 --> 00:18:34.446
- You went back and visited.

00:18:34.446 --> 00:18:35.581
Yeah, after this happened.

00:18:35.581 --> 00:18:36.748
- Yeah, I did.

00:18:36.748 --> 00:18:38.250
And I started working
with the school

00:18:38.250 --> 00:18:39.384
cause a lot of the kids
were going through trauma

00:18:39.384 --> 00:18:41.053
and so they called on alumni.

00:18:41.053 --> 00:18:43.021
But the thing for me
about that incident,

00:18:43.021 --> 00:18:46.291
which a lot of people don't
know is that one of the teachers

00:18:46.291 --> 00:18:50.496
he was protecting the
students and he got killed

00:18:50.496 --> 00:18:53.031
was actually my high school,

00:18:54.099 --> 00:18:55.033
we went to high school together.

00:18:55.033 --> 00:18:56.602
We graduated the same time.

00:18:56.602 --> 00:18:59.471
And so when I watched
it on the news,

00:18:59.471 --> 00:19:02.241
not only was I like feeling
this anxiety of being a father

00:19:02.241 --> 00:19:04.376
with my kids going to
school, also watching

00:19:04.376 --> 00:19:08.447
my high school be shot
up and the kids and,

00:19:08.447 --> 00:19:10.782
seeing their fear and
hiding home there.

00:19:10.782 --> 00:19:12.851
But then a day
later to see someone

00:19:12.851 --> 00:19:14.319
I actually graduated from.

00:19:14.319 --> 00:19:15.754
And I remember when he
sent the Facebook message

00:19:15.754 --> 00:19:17.756
to say he was going
back there to teach,

00:19:18.891 --> 00:19:20.125
and now dead.

00:19:20.125 --> 00:19:22.161
Like, it made me feel
like I was 16 again

00:19:22.161 --> 00:19:23.362
because I'm like,

00:19:23.362 --> 00:19:26.298
ah, too close to home.

00:19:26.298 --> 00:19:27.132
Like, you know.

00:19:27.132 --> 00:19:28.367
- Wow.

00:19:28.367 --> 00:19:30.569
And with conflicting
emotions probably

00:19:30.569 --> 00:19:33.238
about that time in your
life and that experience.

00:19:33.238 --> 00:19:35.641
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, so.

00:19:35.641 --> 00:19:40.646
- When did, you know the
sexual identity aspect

00:19:41.246 --> 00:19:42.881
of growing up,

00:19:42.881 --> 00:19:44.683
come to be a thing.

00:19:44.683 --> 00:19:47.986
I mean, you sort of came to
terms with that around 16 or so?

00:19:47.986 --> 00:19:48.820
- 15.

00:19:50.622 --> 00:19:52.124
It came across very quickly.

00:19:52.124 --> 00:19:55.894
I mean, you know, Zack
Morris on Saved by the Bell,

00:19:55.894 --> 00:19:57.129
helped that a lot.

00:19:57.129 --> 00:19:58.797
I kind of figured
it out very quickly.

00:19:58.797 --> 00:20:00.799
Like, oh, I like boys.

00:20:00.799 --> 00:20:04.970
And so I think the journey
to accepting love myself

00:20:04.970 --> 00:20:06.872
was a difficult one

00:20:06.872 --> 00:20:10.309
because my father was
Jamaican, is Jamaican

00:20:10.309 --> 00:20:15.314
and the music and the
culture previously

00:20:16.181 --> 00:20:17.449
they've gotten a lot better,

00:20:17.449 --> 00:20:18.650
subscribed to a lot
of homophobic ways.

00:20:18.650 --> 00:20:21.086
And so there was a song
called Boom Bye Bye

00:20:21.086 --> 00:20:23.155
by an artist called Buju Banton.

00:20:23.155 --> 00:20:25.657
And the song went like this,

00:20:25.657 --> 00:20:27.626
boom bye bye (speaking
in foreign language),

00:20:27.626 --> 00:20:30.128
which means homosexuals head.

00:20:30.128 --> 00:20:32.197
You're not supposed to
promote these nasty men.

00:20:32.197 --> 00:20:33.432
You have to kill them dead.

00:20:33.432 --> 00:20:35.167
And that song would be playing

00:20:35.167 --> 00:20:37.536
at family functions
consistently.

00:20:37.536 --> 00:20:39.271
And it had a great beat and
people are dancing to it.

00:20:39.271 --> 00:20:42.708
And the whole song is
about killing any gay men

00:20:42.708 --> 00:20:44.810
onsite that are,

00:20:44.810 --> 00:20:47.246
and this was like
number one on the radio,

00:20:47.246 --> 00:20:50.749
and number like 10 on the
radio here in the States.

00:20:50.749 --> 00:20:52.884
So I want you to
imagine a popular song

00:20:52.884 --> 00:20:55.020
that's promoting killing gay men

00:20:55.020 --> 00:20:59.992
and I'm five, seven, eight,
nine, and the songs are playing.

00:21:01.159 --> 00:21:03.996
And so it made me feel
fearful for my own life.

00:21:03.996 --> 00:21:05.130
It made me feel,

00:21:08.667 --> 00:21:11.103
unsure about the love that
my parents really have,

00:21:11.103 --> 00:21:13.071
because if you could
sing that song,

00:21:13.071 --> 00:21:16.875
unknowing that I was gay,
then do you really love me?

00:21:16.875 --> 00:21:19.344
Once I say this to you, are
you gonna try to kill me?

00:21:19.344 --> 00:21:21.146
Those are the types of
things you play in your head.

00:21:21.146 --> 00:21:21.980
- That's horrible man.

00:21:21.980 --> 00:21:23.348
- Yeah, you know.

00:21:23.348 --> 00:21:24.883
But I think about that,

00:21:24.883 --> 00:21:28.854
we still haven't gotten to
a place with some rap pop,

00:21:30.956 --> 00:21:32.491
some rock lyrics,

00:21:32.491 --> 00:21:33.592
they still promote this.

00:21:33.592 --> 00:21:35.227
And people don't realize that,

00:21:35.227 --> 00:21:39.264
you saying no homo has
a connotation to someone

00:21:39.264 --> 00:21:43.835
who could be, who identifies
by the LGBTQ community

00:21:43.835 --> 00:21:45.370
and who was having
self-esteem issues.

00:21:45.370 --> 00:21:46.705
And every time you say no homo,

00:21:46.705 --> 00:21:48.307
how does that affect
their self-esteem?

00:21:48.307 --> 00:21:51.109
Because you're saying
that if I do something

00:21:51.109 --> 00:21:53.845
no homo means it's bad.

00:21:53.845 --> 00:21:55.480
So like, I'm not bad.

00:21:55.480 --> 00:21:56.315
That's bad.

00:21:56.315 --> 00:21:57.616
I'm not bad.

00:21:57.616 --> 00:21:59.518
And so I think it's just
about being clear about

00:21:59.518 --> 00:22:01.119
what are you subscribing to

00:22:01.119 --> 00:22:03.388
and like really
watching your language,

00:22:03.388 --> 00:22:06.291
because language has power of
how it affects people's mood,

00:22:06.291 --> 00:22:08.193
self-esteem, growth, everything.

00:22:08.193 --> 00:22:09.661
- Yeah, wow.

00:22:09.661 --> 00:22:10.829
That's heavy and,

00:22:12.197 --> 00:22:14.599
where are you at with
your dad these days?

00:22:18.870 --> 00:22:20.839
- We have a like,
you're good, I'm good.

00:22:20.839 --> 00:22:23.208
Like, hey, you know,
you stay there.

00:22:23.208 --> 00:22:25.744
The problem is that even,
my father is 70 now,

00:22:25.744 --> 00:22:28.914
we had many years that
we didn't talk at all.

00:22:30.315 --> 00:22:32.918
But then like, as I got
older I did try to reach out

00:22:32.918 --> 00:22:34.419
and he just could not.

00:22:34.419 --> 00:22:36.722
He just still to this
day cannot reconcile

00:22:36.722 --> 00:22:39.458
his religion with his
relationship with his son.

00:22:39.458 --> 00:22:40.559
And I think that's a problem.

00:22:40.559 --> 00:22:42.027
When you can't
reconcile your religion

00:22:42.027 --> 00:22:44.830
with your relationships,
then there's an issue there.

00:22:44.830 --> 00:22:47.132
Because for me, the
religion is teaching you

00:22:47.132 --> 00:22:49.634
to have healthy
relationships through love.

00:22:49.634 --> 00:22:54.072
But somehow, the
Bible is then teaching

00:22:55.273 --> 00:22:58.243
a different version of
what could be coming out

00:22:58.243 --> 00:22:59.878
of other people's now,
there was supposed to be.

00:22:59.878 --> 00:23:01.813
And so he's just never
been able to reconcile it.

00:23:01.813 --> 00:23:03.849
And so I had to come to the
place and say, you know what

00:23:03.849 --> 00:23:06.785
since you can't
reconcile that decision

00:23:06.785 --> 00:23:08.553
I have to make a
decision for myself.

00:23:08.553 --> 00:23:11.356
I have to love me
more than I love you.

00:23:11.356 --> 00:23:14.926
I have to trust me
more than I trust you.

00:23:14.926 --> 00:23:16.695
I have to be there for
myself more than you

00:23:16.695 --> 00:23:17.963
could ever be there for me.

00:23:17.963 --> 00:23:20.565
And then that was sort
of the the first step

00:23:20.565 --> 00:23:22.367
in taking away the
pressure of feeling like

00:23:22.367 --> 00:23:24.336
I have to have this
relationship with my father

00:23:24.336 --> 00:23:25.704
just because he has
the title father.

00:23:25.704 --> 00:23:26.571
- Right, right.

00:23:26.571 --> 00:23:28.039
That's the thing.

00:23:28.039 --> 00:23:32.377
I mean, I feel like most people
have some version of issues

00:23:32.377 --> 00:23:33.879
with their parents,

00:23:33.879 --> 00:23:37.516
and it's the rare case that
somebody can really process it

00:23:37.516 --> 00:23:40.619
and heal from it and get to
the other side of it where

00:23:40.619 --> 00:23:43.688
they're not carrying
around this burdensome,

00:23:43.688 --> 00:23:46.057
resentment and anger,

00:23:46.057 --> 00:23:48.527
constantly looking in
the rear view mirror

00:23:48.527 --> 00:23:50.662
like analyzing what
that experience was

00:23:50.662 --> 00:23:52.497
like that takes a lot of work

00:23:52.497 --> 00:23:55.367
and most people just
compartmentalize it and move on.

00:23:55.367 --> 00:23:57.669
- Well, one of the things
that I used to tell myself

00:23:57.669 --> 00:23:58.904
and I try to help,

00:23:58.904 --> 00:24:00.605
especially when I worked
in social services,

00:24:00.605 --> 00:24:04.910
I would tell kids
as sort of something

00:24:04.910 --> 00:24:08.380
that's springboard them to a
happier healthier life is that

00:24:08.380 --> 00:24:11.249
whatever situation happened,
didn't happen to you,

00:24:11.249 --> 00:24:12.584
it happened for you.

00:24:12.584 --> 00:24:14.453
And I think that
language right there,

00:24:14.453 --> 00:24:17.389
it didn't happen to
me, it happened for me.

00:24:17.389 --> 00:24:20.625
It's such a beautiful way of
helping you to understand that,

00:24:20.625 --> 00:24:22.794
yes, you had an experience
that was traumatic

00:24:22.794 --> 00:24:24.529
but what could you learn
from this experience?

00:24:24.529 --> 00:24:25.464
How can you grow?

00:24:25.464 --> 00:24:26.665
How can you be healthier?

00:24:26.665 --> 00:24:28.133
How can you be the best
version of yourself?

00:24:28.133 --> 00:24:29.601
How can you start to heal?

00:24:29.601 --> 00:24:32.337
Because when you say
it happened to me

00:24:32.337 --> 00:24:35.674
you live in this place of
letting the trauma overtake you.

00:24:35.674 --> 00:24:38.443
It's always like,
this happened to me.

00:24:38.443 --> 00:24:39.611
I wasn't good enough.

00:24:39.611 --> 00:24:41.213
I wasn't strong
enough to handle this.

00:24:41.213 --> 00:24:43.982
What did I do to
allow this to happen?

00:24:43.982 --> 00:24:45.250
Why wasn't I loved enough?

00:24:45.250 --> 00:24:47.018
You start to do all these whys,

00:24:47.018 --> 00:24:49.254
instead of saying
this happened for me

00:24:49.254 --> 00:24:51.857
so that I could understand
that I could be stronger,

00:24:51.857 --> 00:24:53.091
that I could love myself.

00:24:53.091 --> 00:24:54.326
And so when I think
about the relationship

00:24:54.326 --> 00:24:56.495
that I have with my
father and uncles

00:24:56.495 --> 00:24:58.029
who couldn't reconcile
their religion

00:24:58.029 --> 00:25:00.465
with their relationship with
me because of my sexuality,

00:25:00.465 --> 00:25:02.367
I say, you know what
that happened for me

00:25:02.367 --> 00:25:04.569
so that I could be
here today, stronger,

00:25:04.569 --> 00:25:07.205
loving myself more
versus it happened to me.

00:25:07.205 --> 00:25:08.473
- Yeah.

00:25:08.473 --> 00:25:10.075
It's easier to be on
the other side of it

00:25:10.075 --> 00:25:12.577
though and look at it that
way, like intellectually.

00:25:12.577 --> 00:25:13.378
Yeah, of course.

00:25:13.378 --> 00:25:14.713
But when you're in it,

00:25:14.713 --> 00:25:15.947
when you're mired in it,

00:25:15.947 --> 00:25:18.250
it's very hard to like
see your way through

00:25:18.250 --> 00:25:19.584
and to really grab onto that.

00:25:19.584 --> 00:25:21.052
- Oh, 100%.

00:25:21.052 --> 00:25:23.855
That's why I think that they
should teach meditation.

00:25:23.855 --> 00:25:25.090
I think one of the
things that I think

00:25:25.090 --> 00:25:26.725
that they should have
not taken out of school

00:25:26.725 --> 00:25:27.959
was quiet time.

00:25:27.959 --> 00:25:29.194
I think, you know,

00:25:29.194 --> 00:25:30.428
they got it right
in kindergarten.

00:25:30.428 --> 00:25:31.696
In pre-K, when you
have quiet time

00:25:31.696 --> 00:25:33.164
because what would
happen is that

00:25:33.164 --> 00:25:36.234
it would allow all the kids
to settle their nerves,

00:25:36.234 --> 00:25:39.104
to take a moment to
reflect, to recharge.

00:25:39.104 --> 00:25:42.374
And I think we get into this
as a culture, as a society.

00:25:42.374 --> 00:25:44.242
It's like, you have
to go, go, go, go, go.

00:25:44.242 --> 00:25:46.444
And you don't look at
social media, everything

00:25:46.444 --> 00:25:47.712
and people don't slow down.

00:25:47.712 --> 00:25:50.048
And I think that if
we had an opportunity

00:25:50.048 --> 00:25:51.783
for people to take a step back,

00:25:51.783 --> 00:25:53.485
especially in traumatic moments,

00:25:53.485 --> 00:25:55.921
through an organized,

00:25:55.921 --> 00:25:58.089
through school where
they're like, let's stop,

00:25:58.089 --> 00:26:00.358
so you can process
what's going on.

00:26:00.358 --> 00:26:02.727
I think it would allow
people to then get

00:26:02.727 --> 00:26:04.663
to that intellectual
place at a younger age

00:26:04.663 --> 00:26:07.566
versus being older, because
no one allowed me to stop.

00:26:07.566 --> 00:26:09.467
So I felt like I had
to continue to run.

00:26:09.467 --> 00:26:11.069
I felt like I continued
to have to fight

00:26:11.069 --> 00:26:14.739
because of the fact that I
was like, if I stop, they win.

00:26:14.739 --> 00:26:17.342
When actually, if I
would've stopped sooner

00:26:17.342 --> 00:26:19.711
I would have healed
quicker, I would've won.

00:26:19.711 --> 00:26:22.280
Because I would have
more understanding that

00:26:22.280 --> 00:26:24.649
this is your ship,
this is not my ship,

00:26:24.649 --> 00:26:26.685
you know what I mean?

00:26:26.685 --> 00:26:29.554
So that's, I talk about
education a lot and kids.

00:26:29.554 --> 00:26:31.823
- Well, I mean,
there's so many things,

00:26:31.823 --> 00:26:33.325
with both parents.

00:26:33.325 --> 00:26:35.460
I mean, there's so many things
that I wish we could change

00:26:35.460 --> 00:26:37.429
about the educational
system and right now.

00:26:37.429 --> 00:26:38.830
Yeah, of course, thanks.

00:26:38.830 --> 00:26:40.498
In the midst of this pandemic

00:26:40.498 --> 00:26:42.267
when everyone's at home
and on Zoom, I mean,

00:26:42.267 --> 00:26:47.272
prior to this, the incidents
of mental health issues

00:26:48.340 --> 00:26:50.508
among teens was extraordinary.

00:26:50.508 --> 00:26:54.079
Now it's spiking like crazy
depression, anxiety, stress,

00:26:54.079 --> 00:26:56.247
all the like, and there's no

00:26:56.247 --> 00:27:00.619
kind of institutional
programming around well being

00:27:00.619 --> 00:27:03.755
in the educational system,
whether it's private or public.

00:27:03.755 --> 00:27:06.758
Like, my great desire, this,

00:27:06.758 --> 00:27:07.692
and I'll be,

00:27:07.692 --> 00:27:09.060
projecting my own experience.

00:27:09.060 --> 00:27:12.330
But, I want my
children, my daughters

00:27:12.330 --> 00:27:15.634
to emerge from their
educations feeling,

00:27:15.634 --> 00:27:18.737
a sense of empowerment
and self-esteem

00:27:18.737 --> 00:27:20.405
and agency over their lives

00:27:20.405 --> 00:27:23.842
and a feeling of capability
that they can manage the world.

00:27:23.842 --> 00:27:25.677
Like all of these life skills,

00:27:25.677 --> 00:27:30.015
there's so much more
important than memorization or

00:27:30.015 --> 00:27:32.450
whatever being tested this week.

00:27:32.450 --> 00:27:33.652
- I agree.

00:27:33.652 --> 00:27:35.587
- We just completely
turn a blind eye

00:27:35.587 --> 00:27:38.390
to this fundamental aspect of,

00:27:38.390 --> 00:27:41.059
how to live successfully
and be happy

00:27:41.059 --> 00:27:42.694
and productive and fulfilled.

00:27:42.694 --> 00:27:44.863
- Yeah, and I think that's
part of the reason why,

00:27:44.863 --> 00:27:49.668
as American culture, like we
are not number one anymore.

00:27:49.668 --> 00:27:50.602
You know what I mean?

00:27:50.602 --> 00:27:51.836
Because we are missing such

00:27:51.836 --> 00:27:54.406
a key fundamental
piece here like,

00:27:54.406 --> 00:27:56.941
people need education, they
do need the memorization,

00:27:56.941 --> 00:27:58.410
they need to go those things.

00:27:58.410 --> 00:28:01.913
But that's 25% of what has
made me successful in my life.

00:28:01.913 --> 00:28:04.549
You know, like the skills
I learned in school,

00:28:04.549 --> 00:28:06.284
obviously, yes, I have them

00:28:06.284 --> 00:28:08.286
but it's through the
life experiences.

00:28:08.286 --> 00:28:09.888
And it's through,
like you just said

00:28:09.888 --> 00:28:12.590
having agency over myself
and over my emotions

00:28:12.590 --> 00:28:14.993
and being able to
communicate and being able

00:28:14.993 --> 00:28:16.327
to take care of my mental health

00:28:16.327 --> 00:28:18.263
and being able to
do all these things

00:28:18.263 --> 00:28:20.799
