And I was like, that
just blew my mind.

00:31:04.596 --> 00:31:06.865
But so I take the
responsibility.

00:31:06.865 --> 00:31:09.801
It's very important to me.

00:31:09.801 --> 00:31:13.471
I love it because I
hopefully we're giving people

00:31:13.471 --> 00:31:16.341
and hopefully I'm giving people
ways of really checking in

00:31:16.341 --> 00:31:19.510
with themselves that are
not so much over their head.

00:31:19.510 --> 00:31:21.012
I think a lot of
times these concepts

00:31:21.012 --> 00:31:22.247
can go over people's heads.

00:31:22.247 --> 00:31:24.015
So I always try to
like break it down.

00:31:24.015 --> 00:31:25.617
Not because I think
people need things

00:31:25.617 --> 00:31:28.553
to be dumbed down to them, but
sometimes in our busy lives

00:31:28.553 --> 00:31:29.754
you just need something
that you're like,

00:31:29.754 --> 00:31:30.688
oh, I can grab this.

00:31:30.688 --> 00:31:32.190
- The one thing that I can do.

00:31:32.190 --> 00:31:33.358
- Yeah, I can do this.

00:31:33.358 --> 00:31:36.461
You know, like not
something that's so big

00:31:36.461 --> 00:31:38.363
that you're like, I can't,
it's gonna consume me.

00:31:38.363 --> 00:31:40.031
Just do this one thing.

00:31:40.031 --> 00:31:41.132
Here you go.

00:31:41.132 --> 00:31:43.101
Let's start with this.

00:31:43.101 --> 00:31:48.072
- Well, as they say in the
secret halls of recovery

00:31:49.507 --> 00:31:50.842
you can't transmit something
you haven't got, right?

00:31:50.842 --> 00:31:52.377
- Amen.

00:31:52.377 --> 00:31:54.245
- And that goes back to what
you were saying earlier about,

00:31:54.245 --> 00:31:56.781
is this happening to you
or is it happening for you?

00:31:56.781 --> 00:31:58.983
Like when you can
imbue that idea

00:31:58.983 --> 00:32:00.618
that it's happening for you

00:32:00.618 --> 00:32:03.988
and avail yourself of
the opportunity to,

00:32:03.988 --> 00:32:08.192
really like bear hug it and
use it as a modality to heal

00:32:08.192 --> 00:32:10.328
as opposed to become a victim,

00:32:10.328 --> 00:32:12.130
you can emerge
stronger from that.

00:32:12.130 --> 00:32:14.999
And you know, you've only
shared a few of the many things

00:32:14.999 --> 00:32:16.501
that you've had to do.

00:32:16.501 --> 00:32:17.702
I mean, you had, like, you
found out you had a son

00:32:17.702 --> 00:32:19.604
when you were in
your early twenties.

00:32:19.604 --> 00:32:21.239
Almost your age.

00:32:21.239 --> 00:32:22.941
- And it is funny.

00:32:22.941 --> 00:32:25.677
We were in the car
literally three days ago.

00:32:25.677 --> 00:32:27.512
And he was like, just
sitting there randomly.

00:32:27.512 --> 00:32:30.915
He was like, when
you're 65, I'll be 50.

00:32:30.915 --> 00:32:33.318
And I was like, yeah.

00:32:33.318 --> 00:32:35.586
And he was like, no one's
gonna think you're my dad.

00:32:35.586 --> 00:32:38.256
And I was like, I
mean, it happens now,

00:32:38.256 --> 00:32:39.624
people think we're brothers,
you know what I mean?

00:32:39.624 --> 00:32:42.727
But yeah, the one
girl that I had,

00:32:42.727 --> 00:32:44.429
she was my best friend.

00:32:44.429 --> 00:32:45.930
We lost our virginity
to each other.

00:32:45.930 --> 00:32:49.634
It was also the catalyst for
me to start letting people

00:32:49.634 --> 00:32:51.436
into my life in regards
to my sexuality.

00:32:51.436 --> 00:32:53.204
It was like, okay,

00:32:53.204 --> 00:32:54.772
I know this is not for me.

00:32:56.240 --> 00:32:59.610
And so it was one of those
things where I was like,

00:32:59.610 --> 00:33:00.778
yeah, I guess this is it.

00:33:00.778 --> 00:33:03.414
And then she moved
away, you know, I'm 40.

00:33:03.414 --> 00:33:06.351
And so people who are
younger might not get this

00:33:06.351 --> 00:33:09.220
but if you didn't have someone's
address or homeless phone.

00:33:09.220 --> 00:33:10.054
- That was it.

00:33:10.054 --> 00:33:11.255
- That was it.

00:33:11.255 --> 00:33:12.690
And so once she
moved, it was done.

00:33:12.690 --> 00:33:14.726
And then years later,
there's that stack of papers

00:33:14.726 --> 00:33:16.094
on my doorstep for (indistinct).

00:33:16.094 --> 00:33:17.295
- You didn't know
that she got pregnant.

00:33:17.295 --> 00:33:18.129
She moved away.

00:33:18.129 --> 00:33:18.963
- She moved away.

00:33:19.931 --> 00:33:21.866
She got pregnant in,

00:33:21.866 --> 00:33:23.768
if I do the math back,
I guess it was like May

00:33:23.768 --> 00:33:25.603
and then we were out
of school by June.

00:33:25.603 --> 00:33:26.838
So we went on summer break,

00:33:26.838 --> 00:33:28.773
and then she moved
during summer break.

00:33:28.773 --> 00:33:29.841
So that was,

00:33:29.841 --> 00:33:31.376
yeah, that was it.

00:33:31.376 --> 00:33:33.745
- So your son was 10 when
you were informed, right?

00:33:33.745 --> 00:33:35.113
- Yeah, 10 years old.

00:33:35.113 --> 00:33:36.481
And I was like,
what in the world?

00:33:36.481 --> 00:33:39.150
And at this point I
had been going through,

00:33:39.150 --> 00:33:42.587
I mean, it was like
party LA central.

00:33:42.587 --> 00:33:44.288
I just got off the real world.

00:33:44.288 --> 00:33:45.490
I'm done with college,

00:33:46.657 --> 00:33:48.559
the real world back
then, it was like,

00:33:48.559 --> 00:33:50.028
if you're on reality television.

00:33:50.028 --> 00:33:51.963
I don't know if it's same now,
but back then, it was like

00:33:51.963 --> 00:33:54.665
you would do these club tours
and you would go to tours

00:33:54.665 --> 00:33:56.934
and they would pay
you $20,000 to party

00:33:56.934 --> 00:33:58.569
when I would have
partied for free.

00:33:58.569 --> 00:34:01.672
And so it's like, I'm
23 partying, doing
all these things.

00:34:01.672 --> 00:34:02.607
They're giving you drugs.

00:34:02.607 --> 00:34:04.575
They're just taking care of you.

00:34:04.575 --> 00:34:06.878
And I just got really
unhealthy, really depressed.

00:34:06.878 --> 00:34:08.646
And it was because I
didn't really focus

00:34:08.646 --> 00:34:10.348
on the trauma from my past.

00:34:10.348 --> 00:34:12.283
I wasn't looking at
what was making me feel

00:34:12.283 --> 00:34:13.918
like this behavior was okay.

00:34:13.918 --> 00:34:15.787
Why was it okay for
me to destroy myself?

00:34:15.787 --> 00:34:20.291
Why was it okay, why was
I so okay with hurting me?

00:34:20.291 --> 00:34:24.095
And so as my son, I
found the paperwork.

00:34:24.095 --> 00:34:26.531
It was at the same time when
I was finally starting to heal

00:34:26.531 --> 00:34:28.399
and saying, you know
what enough is enough.

00:34:28.399 --> 00:34:30.435
I don't wanna hurt me anymore.

00:34:30.435 --> 00:34:31.836
The world does
enough of hurting me.

00:34:31.836 --> 00:34:33.404
I don't wanna hurt me anymore.

00:34:33.404 --> 00:34:37.809
And so I started to heal,
get help for my addictions.

00:34:37.809 --> 00:34:40.111
And then now it
was like this kid

00:34:40.111 --> 00:34:41.512
and it was the perfect thing.

00:34:41.512 --> 00:34:43.247
I tell people all the
time, he saved my life

00:34:43.247 --> 00:34:46.651
because I don't know
what would have happened

00:34:46.651 --> 00:34:49.454
if I would've found out
at 16 that I was a father.

00:34:49.454 --> 00:34:52.090
I don't know if I'd be sitting
here right now, I don't know.

00:34:52.090 --> 00:34:54.859
And I don't like to
speculate, but I do know that

00:34:54.859 --> 00:34:58.763
at that moment I needed
something ground,

00:34:58.763 --> 00:35:03.701
life-changing to step in and
like really make me commit

00:35:05.136 --> 00:35:06.671
to who I wanted to be and
to stop hurting myself.

00:35:06.671 --> 00:35:08.139
- Yeah, the universe delivered.

00:35:08.139 --> 00:35:09.607
- Delivered, right on my doors.

00:35:09.607 --> 00:35:10.942
- Timing was perfect.

00:35:10.942 --> 00:35:12.376
- Yeah, on my doorstep.

00:35:12.376 --> 00:35:15.046
And so I moved back to
Texas to get custody of him.

00:35:15.913 --> 00:35:17.882
It was, you know, crazy moment.

00:35:17.882 --> 00:35:20.284
I then got custody of
his little brother,

00:35:20.284 --> 00:35:21.786
same mother, different fathers

00:35:21.786 --> 00:35:24.388
because he was having some
troubles with the state.

00:35:24.388 --> 00:35:26.124
And because of my,

00:35:26.124 --> 00:35:27.692
work in social
services, I was like,

00:35:27.692 --> 00:35:29.093
I can take him for temporary.

00:35:29.093 --> 00:35:30.995
And temporary turned into,

00:35:30.995 --> 00:35:32.697
you know, he's 20 now.

00:35:32.697 --> 00:35:34.065
He (indistinct) time.

00:35:35.166 --> 00:35:38.035
And so it, but it helped
me understand like

00:35:38.035 --> 00:35:40.938
oh, you can be better knowing
it for yourself but for them.

00:35:40.938 --> 00:35:43.441
- So you're like 26, 27

00:35:43.441 --> 00:35:45.042
and you're like I'm adopting.
- 27.

00:35:45.042 --> 00:35:47.178
- I got a son and I'm
adopting this other kid.

00:35:47.178 --> 00:35:50.581
And now I'm a single dad of two,

00:35:50.581 --> 00:35:53.818
leaving LA and my fancy
TV real-world life

00:35:53.818 --> 00:35:55.586
(indistinct) back to Texas.

00:35:55.586 --> 00:35:56.420
- It wasn't that fancy.

00:35:56.420 --> 00:35:57.922
It wasn't that fancy.

00:35:57.922 --> 00:36:00.057
- It's fancy for most
people, 20 grand to go party.

00:36:00.057 --> 00:36:01.125
That sounds like pretty good.

00:36:01.125 --> 00:36:02.326
- And this is the funny part.

00:36:02.326 --> 00:36:03.528
I would get 20
down and go party.

00:36:03.528 --> 00:36:06.531
And then maybe three
days later, I'd be like,

00:36:06.531 --> 00:36:08.733
how's my phone bill
getting turned off.

00:36:08.733 --> 00:36:10.701
And I was realizing
because you're 23

00:36:10.701 --> 00:36:13.538
and you don't know
how to manage $20,000.

00:36:13.538 --> 00:36:15.606
So it was like, hey,
everybody get into,

00:36:15.606 --> 00:36:17.208
you know we're all,
I'm buying a trip.

00:36:17.208 --> 00:36:18.476
We're all going.

00:36:18.476 --> 00:36:19.710
And then, oh, you
want those shoes?

00:36:19.710 --> 00:36:21.179
Sure, get those shoes,
we're all doing it.

00:36:21.179 --> 00:36:22.980
And then you're like, oh,
because I didn't know.

00:36:22.980 --> 00:36:24.182
You know so.

00:36:24.182 --> 00:36:26.484
- So did you just
like get struck sober

00:36:26.484 --> 00:36:29.453
or did you go to like
12 step or treatment,

00:36:29.453 --> 00:36:30.288
how did you do that?

00:36:30.288 --> 00:36:31.489
- I did not.

00:36:31.489 --> 00:36:34.258
I did it on my own,
which I regret.

00:36:34.258 --> 00:36:38.062
I wish that I would've
had someone to guide me

00:36:38.062 --> 00:36:41.499
but my family, especially
in African-American cultures

00:36:41.499 --> 00:36:46.504
and immigrant cultures,
therapy, addiction recovery

00:36:47.672 --> 00:36:49.540
is not something that
they're educated on.

00:36:49.540 --> 00:36:52.009
And a lot of times and I'm
not trying to generalize,

00:36:52.009 --> 00:36:54.845
but from my experience,
the community that was in

00:36:54.845 --> 00:36:56.881
of African-American
people or immigrants

00:36:56.881 --> 00:37:01.018
just did not know about therapy,
did not know about rehab,

00:37:01.018 --> 00:37:02.853
didn't have the money
or the resources.

00:37:02.853 --> 00:37:04.722
And so it was sort of
like this thing of like,

00:37:04.722 --> 00:37:07.858
pray on it, you know
like, or just stop.

00:37:07.858 --> 00:37:10.328
- But you study this
stuff in college, right?

00:37:10.328 --> 00:37:12.597
Like you were a psychotherapist
and a social worker.

00:37:12.597 --> 00:37:14.065
Like you must have had some.

00:37:14.065 --> 00:37:15.366
- Let me tell you something.

00:37:15.366 --> 00:37:17.501
This is the thing it's
funny, Chris and I,

00:37:17.501 --> 00:37:20.404
there's a guy here named
Chris that I'm dating.

00:37:20.404 --> 00:37:21.872
- He might or might not be
sitting right behind you.

00:37:21.872 --> 00:37:23.074
- Yeah, right behind me.

00:37:24.508 --> 00:37:26.244
But we were just having
this conversation because

00:37:26.244 --> 00:37:29.880
it's like sometimes I'm great
at helping every other person,

00:37:29.880 --> 00:37:32.450
but you don't
understand how fast

00:37:32.450 --> 00:37:35.786
that training goes out of
my mind when it comes to me.

00:37:35.786 --> 00:37:37.221
- There it is.

00:37:37.221 --> 00:37:39.557
- It just goes and anybody
who tells you like,

00:37:39.557 --> 00:37:40.725
oh no, I've been trained.

00:37:40.725 --> 00:37:42.193
And I know how to just do,

00:37:42.193 --> 00:37:43.694
this is why doctors don't
perform on themselves

00:37:43.694 --> 00:37:46.197
or why lawyers don't represent
themselves in court because

00:37:46.197 --> 00:37:47.865
you sometimes don't
advocate for yourself

00:37:47.865 --> 00:37:49.000
in the way you need to.

00:37:49.867 --> 00:37:51.068
Yes, I have the knowledge.

00:37:51.068 --> 00:37:52.603
I always look back and I'm like

00:37:52.603 --> 00:37:54.472
if somebody would have
been in front of me,

00:37:54.472 --> 00:37:56.607
I would have known exactly
what to say to them.

00:37:56.607 --> 00:37:57.842
But when it's you,

00:37:57.842 --> 00:37:59.443
I was like, I don't
know what to do here.

00:37:59.443 --> 00:38:00.945
Like, oh, I'm screwed.

00:38:00.945 --> 00:38:04.282
And so it, the knowledge
helps to some degree

00:38:04.282 --> 00:38:08.019
once I got to a place
where I was kinda better

00:38:08.019 --> 00:38:09.954
but for the most part,
it didn't help at all.

00:38:09.954 --> 00:38:13.257
Because I was like, I was
in such a destructive path.

00:38:13.257 --> 00:38:14.525
You know what I mean?

00:38:14.525 --> 00:38:16.594
Like I just could not
get out of my own way.

00:38:16.594 --> 00:38:18.796
I was so complacent
with hurting me,

00:38:18.796 --> 00:38:20.464
and I don't mean
physically hurting me,

00:38:20.464 --> 00:38:22.466
but I just realized
that every choice I made

00:38:22.466 --> 00:38:25.636
was always going to
leave me to something

00:38:25.636 --> 00:38:27.972
that put me in a situation
that I was uncomfortable with.

00:38:27.972 --> 00:38:30.608
It put me in relationships
that I shouldn't have been in,

00:38:30.608 --> 00:38:32.376
both friendships and intimate.

00:38:32.376 --> 00:38:35.179
Every decision I was making
was about hurting me.

00:38:35.179 --> 00:38:36.447
It was never conscious.

00:38:37.615 --> 00:38:39.850
It was until I got a
little bit more sober

00:38:39.850 --> 00:38:41.385
that I was able to evaluate that

00:38:41.385 --> 00:38:45.489
to understand how the drugs and
alcohol were triggering that

00:38:45.489 --> 00:38:47.792
what trauma I was experiencing.

00:38:47.792 --> 00:38:51.095
It was a lot of why,
how that I went through.

00:38:51.095 --> 00:38:54.498
- But at some point you must
have enlisted other people

00:38:54.498 --> 00:38:56.500
to help guide you
through that process.

00:38:56.500 --> 00:38:59.403
You weren't all doing this
in your head, were you?

00:38:59.403 --> 00:39:00.237
- Kinda.

00:39:00.237 --> 00:39:01.038
- Wow.

00:39:01.038 --> 00:39:02.506
- I wish that I could,

00:39:02.506 --> 00:39:04.041
because I have one sister,
her name is Camillia.

00:39:04.041 --> 00:39:06.444
She and I are the closest
out of my sisters.

00:39:06.444 --> 00:39:08.913
She was the only one
that was like, come here

00:39:08.913 --> 00:39:10.181
but she didn't
have the language.

00:39:10.181 --> 00:39:13.250
So she is a doctor of pharmacy.

00:39:13.250 --> 00:39:16.354
And so she kinda understood
the drugs I was taking

00:39:16.354 --> 00:39:17.955
and she would be like,
she would come to me.

00:39:17.955 --> 00:39:19.757
And these are things that
I already kinda knew.

00:39:19.757 --> 00:39:22.360
- Let me guess, like
cocaine, for sure.

00:39:22.360 --> 00:39:23.427
- You loved cocaine.

00:39:24.595 --> 00:39:25.996
Like, that would be
an understatement.

00:39:25.996 --> 00:39:27.798
I'm not promoting this
to anybody watching.

00:39:27.798 --> 00:39:29.066
- Ecstasy, MDMA.

00:39:29.066 --> 00:39:31.969
- Ecstasy, coke, weed.

00:39:31.969 --> 00:39:35.373
I never did like any heroin
crack or anything like that,

00:39:37.041 --> 00:39:38.943
but it was a lot of like pills,

00:39:39.810 --> 00:39:41.312
anything and then the liquor.

00:39:41.312 --> 00:39:43.748
And then some nights it'd
be all of them because,

00:39:43.748 --> 00:39:47.184
you'd get to the
party at 10 or 11

00:39:47.184 --> 00:39:48.319
and you'd start
off with cocaine.

00:39:48.319 --> 00:39:50.721
And then by one,
someone gives you a pill

00:39:50.721 --> 00:39:52.256
and then by,

00:39:52.256 --> 00:39:53.924
you're drinking through that
and then you have more coke

00:39:53.924 --> 00:39:55.126
and then you get more pills.

00:39:55.126 --> 00:39:58.095
And so she was
there to say, hey,

00:39:58.095 --> 00:40:01.232
this is how your body chemistry
is reacting to these drugs

00:40:01.232 --> 00:40:02.633
which was also kind
of nice for me to hear

00:40:02.633 --> 00:40:04.268
because it was like,
okay, now I'm seeing

00:40:04.268 --> 00:40:06.971
how it's like playing into
my depression, my anxiety.

00:40:06.971 --> 00:40:09.340
And that also made
me realize, like,

00:40:09.340 --> 00:40:10.574
I don't have to be depressed.

00:40:10.574 --> 00:40:12.009
I don't have to be anxious.

00:40:12.009 --> 00:40:15.679
I can relieve this substance
and then do more work

00:40:15.679 --> 00:40:18.149
and relieve myself of
some of these feelings.

00:40:18.149 --> 00:40:19.316
And so that was nice.

00:40:19.316 --> 00:40:20.251
But she was the only one kinda--

00:40:20.251 --> 00:40:22.353
- It's a interesting like level

00:40:22.353 --> 00:40:25.389
of self-awareness and maturity

00:40:26.824 --> 00:40:29.493
for somebody who's kind of
doing it internally, right?

00:40:29.493 --> 00:40:32.062
Like not doing it in
a structured modality.

00:40:32.062 --> 00:40:33.798
But I suppose,

00:40:33.798 --> 00:40:37.401
God's like, dude, you're
like way off the reservation.

00:40:37.401 --> 00:40:40.304
I'm gonna throw these
two kids at you.

00:40:40.304 --> 00:40:41.272
- Exactly.

00:40:41.272 --> 00:40:42.673
- And you need to pay attention.

00:40:42.673 --> 00:40:43.874
Like if this doesn't
get your attention,

00:40:43.874 --> 00:40:44.909
I don't know what to do.

00:40:44.909 --> 00:40:46.210
- Well, that's what it was.

00:40:46.210 --> 00:40:47.044
That's why I say the
kids saved me a lot.

00:40:47.044 --> 00:40:48.245
Cause I was like,

00:40:48.245 --> 00:40:49.513
if I can't pay
attention to myself,

00:40:49.513 --> 00:40:50.981
then how am I gonna
pay attention to them?

00:40:50.981 --> 00:40:53.717
You then just pass
on these trauma bonds

00:40:53.717 --> 00:40:55.419
and these generational wounds.

00:40:55.419 --> 00:40:57.488
And like, and instead
of like saying,

00:40:57.488 --> 00:40:58.989
hey, before you have a kid,

00:40:58.989 --> 00:41:01.625
you actually need to get a
license to heal yourself a bit.

00:41:01.625 --> 00:41:02.893
You know what I mean?

00:41:02.893 --> 00:41:04.328
And we're always a
work in progress,

00:41:04.328 --> 00:41:06.630
but I just think it's
interesting that as adults,

00:41:06.630 --> 00:41:08.098
we end up trying
to heal ourselves

00:41:08.098 --> 00:41:09.366
while we're trying to
raise somebody else.

00:41:09.366 --> 00:41:10.868
And then, they're now adults

00:41:10.868 --> 00:41:12.136
and then now the
light bulb clicked

00:41:12.136 --> 00:41:13.471
cause we're in our
thirties or forties

00:41:13.471 --> 00:41:14.872
and we're like, oh crap.

00:41:14.872 --> 00:41:17.408
I should have said this
to you when you were 10.

00:41:17.408 --> 00:41:18.876
- I mean you know,

00:41:18.876 --> 00:41:22.146
I've done so much work to grow
from the person that I was

00:41:22.146 --> 00:41:23.547
into the person that I am now.

00:41:23.547 --> 00:41:25.616
But it is remarkable.

00:41:25.616 --> 00:41:29.720
The extent to which
I continue to repeat,

00:41:29.720 --> 00:41:32.356
these habitual patterns
that are reflective

00:41:32.356 --> 00:41:33.657
of things that my parents did.

00:41:33.657 --> 00:41:35.893
I mean, I think so many of us,

00:41:35.893 --> 00:41:38.929
we parent in opposition to
the way that we're parented.

00:41:38.929 --> 00:41:40.698
Like we have some
wound, and we're like

00:41:40.698 --> 00:41:43.133
I'm never gonna be
like that with my kid,

00:41:43.133 --> 00:41:45.069
but we kind of go too far
in the other direction.

00:41:45.069 --> 00:41:48.906
And I found myself
nonetheless despite adopting

00:41:48.906 --> 00:41:52.543
that mentality perpetuating
something that I didn't like

00:41:52.543 --> 00:41:54.044
about the way that
I was parented

00:41:54.044 --> 00:41:55.913
because it's so
hardwired into me.

00:41:55.913 --> 00:41:57.181
- [Karamo] So hardwired.

00:41:57.181 --> 00:41:58.516
And the only way
that I can become,

00:41:58.516 --> 00:42:00.885
that I'm made aware of it is

00:42:00.885 --> 00:42:03.487
for example if Julie
reflects it back to me

00:42:03.487 --> 00:42:05.489
or I do therapy
with a group of guys

00:42:05.489 --> 00:42:06.790
and I'll share something
and they'll be like,

00:42:06.790 --> 00:42:08.158
you're doing it again.

00:42:08.158 --> 00:42:09.727
And I was like really, how am I?

00:42:09.727 --> 00:42:11.662
I still can't see it.

00:42:11.662 --> 00:42:16.033
And so these kids become
your gurus, right?

00:42:16.033 --> 00:42:18.068
Because they
reflect back to you,

00:42:18.068 --> 00:42:20.371
your wounding and your
pain and your patterns

00:42:20.371 --> 00:42:23.407
and what it is that
you need to work on.

00:42:23.407 --> 00:42:26.110
And that seems to be a reservoir

00:42:26.110 --> 00:42:27.578
that you never
get to the bottom.

00:42:27.578 --> 00:42:29.046
- Yeah, but if
you're a defensive

00:42:29.046 --> 00:42:30.481
when they're mirroring it back,

00:42:30.481 --> 00:42:33.217
then that's where you start
to pass on that trauma

00:42:33.217 --> 00:42:35.252
and those wounds, because
then they start to pick it up.

00:42:35.252 --> 00:42:36.554
Because once you're
defensive, they're like,

00:42:36.554 --> 00:42:38.389
well, okay, dad does
wanna hear this.

00:42:38.389 --> 00:42:39.390
Mom doesn't wanna hear this.

00:42:39.390 --> 00:42:40.424
And then they start to pick up

00:42:40.424 --> 00:42:42.393
the same behavior unconsciously.

00:42:42.393 --> 00:42:44.428
I was talking...

00:42:44.428 --> 00:42:45.663
I read some of the other day

00:42:45.663 --> 00:42:47.197
which I thought was
just really great

00:42:47.197 --> 00:42:48.832
that I wanna share is how,

00:42:50.267 --> 00:42:53.137
those of us who were kids
and experienced trauma

00:42:53.137 --> 00:42:55.005
or were teenagers
or young adults

00:42:55.005 --> 00:42:56.874
and experienced
addiction or trauma,

00:42:56.874 --> 00:43:00.644
you have learned how to
turn red flags green.

00:43:00.644 --> 00:43:03.180
And I thought that was so great,

00:43:03.180 --> 00:43:06.317
when you're in this space
of dealing with your trauma,

00:43:06.317 --> 00:43:07.885
there's a red flag that pops up

00:43:07.885 --> 00:43:09.920
when you're dealing with your
kids or dealing with life

00:43:09.920 --> 00:43:12.356
where you know that
something is not right here

00:43:12.356 --> 00:43:14.925
but you've turned that
red flag into a green flag

00:43:14.925 --> 00:43:16.493
and giving yourself
permission to go.

00:43:16.493 --> 00:43:18.429
And I think that if
we can start to learn

00:43:18.429 --> 00:43:22.967
how to stop turning these
red flags into green flags

00:43:22.967 --> 00:43:25.135
and teaching our kids how
to stop turning red flags

00:43:25.135 --> 00:43:29.540
into green flags, it helps
them to find the courage

00:43:29.540 --> 00:43:32.643
and the strength
to be able to say,

00:43:32.643 --> 00:43:34.511
this behavior is not okay,

00:43:34.511 --> 00:43:36.647
and I'm not gonna
go along with it.

00:43:36.647 --> 00:43:38.148
I'm not gonna repeat it.

00:43:38.148 --> 00:43:39.917
I'm not going to do
any of the things

00:43:39.917 --> 00:43:43.320
that I've been taught to do,
because I can see the red flag.

00:43:43.320 --> 00:43:47.758
And I think that visual of
like someone taking a red flag,

00:43:47.758 --> 00:43:49.326
like, whew, whew, don't go here,

00:43:49.326 --> 00:43:51.695
and being like, let
me paint this green.

00:43:51.695 --> 00:43:53.897
I'm gonna go now, this is safe.

00:43:53.897 --> 00:43:56.867
I think it's just such a great
visual for us to hold onto

00:43:56.867 --> 00:43:58.902
as we're told.

00:43:58.902 --> 00:44:01.105
As someone reflects back to
us, something that we're doing

00:44:01.105 --> 00:44:04.208
where we can say, oh,
you reflected it back.

00:44:04.208 --> 00:44:06.510
Great, let's all remember
this is a red flag.

00:44:06.510 --> 00:44:08.479
None of us turn this
green, none of us.

00:44:08.479 --> 00:44:09.713
You don't do it.

00:44:09.713 --> 00:44:10.914
You don't do it
in a relationship.

00:44:10.914 --> 00:44:12.483
And if you feel like
you're about to,

00:44:12.483 --> 00:44:15.452
tell everyone in your circle,
what red flags is there it is.

00:44:15.452 --> 00:44:18.389
Instead of like being
in a relationship
and then being like,

00:44:18.389 --> 00:44:21.525
oh, I got to find out two years
later all the red flags when

00:44:21.525 --> 00:44:23.260
be a little bit more honest
and vulnerable about it.

00:44:23.260 --> 00:44:24.862
- Right, right.

00:44:24.862 --> 00:44:27.197
Yeah, I mean the,

00:44:27.197 --> 00:44:29.867
early days of any relationship,

00:44:29.867 --> 00:44:32.903
is basically like a
grand play (indistinct).

00:44:34.071 --> 00:44:34.905
- Pretend.

00:44:34.905 --> 00:44:36.140
We are all pretending.

00:44:36.140 --> 00:44:38.008
- We're modeling this
person we think that

00:44:38.008 --> 00:44:42.012
they wanna be with and it
takes time before the cracks,

00:44:42.012 --> 00:44:43.514
start to showing up.

00:44:43.514 --> 00:44:44.982
- But could you imagine
if we got to a space where

00:44:44.982 --> 00:44:46.316
we could just give those
red flags at the beginning.

00:44:46.316 --> 00:44:48.152
And then how you can
start to heal yourself

00:44:48.152 --> 00:44:50.521
and heal your relationships
so much quicker.

00:44:50.521 --> 00:44:52.189
I just think that's
just a beautiful,

00:44:52.189 --> 00:44:54.758
I mean, it's a little lofty
and like probably won't happen

00:44:54.758 --> 00:44:57.394
but it's great
that I'm trying to,

00:44:57.394 --> 00:44:59.163
I wanna learn how to
practice that more.

00:44:59.163 --> 00:45:00.297
- Yeah, it's hard.

00:45:00.297 --> 00:45:02.866
I'm such a pathological
people pleaser.

00:45:02.866 --> 00:45:04.468
And it's like, I
mean even today,

00:45:04.468 --> 00:45:05.636
it's like Karamo is
coming over here.

00:45:05.636 --> 00:45:08.572
Like I want Karamo to like me,

00:45:08.572 --> 00:45:09.807
you know what I mean?

00:45:09.807 --> 00:45:11.008
Like, I am very
invested in that.

00:45:11.008 --> 00:45:13.143
- So you're seventh
grader too, same thing.

00:45:13.143 --> 00:45:16.313
- And I'm like, what
is that about, like.

00:45:16.313 --> 00:45:21.318
So for me, it's still
this struggle of
trying to detach from

00:45:22.853 --> 00:45:26.990
that aspect of my
personality which I accept,

00:45:26.990 --> 00:45:28.192
but I'm always,

00:45:28.192 --> 00:45:30.694
trying to overcome
on some level,

00:45:30.694 --> 00:45:32.496
which is a close cousin to,

00:45:34.498 --> 00:45:37.201
external validation and
the role that that plays.

00:45:37.201 --> 00:45:38.736
It's like doing a podcast,

00:45:38.736 --> 00:45:40.204
lots of people are
gonna listen to it.

00:45:40.204 --> 00:45:42.172
I like the fact that, you
know what I mean, like

00:45:42.172 --> 00:45:43.507
what aspect of that is fine

00:45:43.507 --> 00:45:46.310
and what aspect of
that becomes unhealthy

00:45:46.310 --> 00:45:49.480
and moves you
further apart from,

00:45:49.480 --> 00:45:50.948
the truth that you're
trying to get at

00:45:50.948 --> 00:45:52.416
in these conversations.

00:45:52.416 --> 00:45:54.418
- Yeah, I think for me,
anytime I'm in those spaces

00:45:54.418 --> 00:45:55.719
I got to think to myself,

00:45:57.020 --> 00:45:59.690
am I doing this because
I know that I'm enough

00:45:59.690 --> 00:46:02.493
or am I doing this because
I don't believe I'm enough.

00:46:02.493 --> 00:46:04.661
And so that validation
is more important to me.

00:46:04.661 --> 00:46:07.364
And I think just
practicing that awareness

00:46:07.364 --> 00:46:09.767
that I am enough, I am okay.

00:46:09.767 --> 00:46:11.835
Sort of is the fine line for me

00:46:11.835 --> 00:46:14.371
of like remembering that,

00:46:14.371 --> 00:46:17.407
yes, I might still have some
of that people pleasing in me

00:46:17.407 --> 00:46:20.444
but the more you can
realize that, you're enough

00:46:20.444 --> 00:46:22.479
so you don't have to
please other people.

00:46:22.479 --> 00:46:25.516
You're enough, so even
if the validation comes

00:46:25.516 --> 00:46:27.818
or doesn't come, it
doesn't matter matter

00:46:27.818 --> 00:46:28.752
because you're enough.

00:46:28.752 --> 00:46:30.754
And I think that's the practice.

00:46:30.754 --> 00:46:33.056
That's the daily
reminder for yourself.

00:46:33.056 --> 00:46:37.294
That's what you
have to hug on to,

00:46:37.294 --> 00:46:39.129
and, you know, hook into.

00:46:39.129 --> 00:46:40.364
Because when you
start to believe

00:46:40.364 --> 00:46:42.566
that it seems very like woo hoo.

00:46:42.566 --> 00:46:45.302
But it's at the truth
of the core of like,

00:46:45.302 --> 00:46:47.938
I don't have to please
you because I'm enough.

00:46:47.938 --> 00:46:49.206
I don't have,

00:46:49.206 --> 00:46:50.808
like it's funny that
you would say like,

00:46:50.808 --> 00:46:52.843
you're thinking like,
I might gonna like you,

00:46:52.843 --> 00:46:54.144
when I'm the one who hit you up

00:46:54.144 --> 00:46:55.646
and told you that I loved you.

00:46:55.646 --> 00:46:57.147
You know what I mean?

00:46:57.147 --> 00:47:00.117
But it's something that we
all play with in our minds.

00:47:00.117 --> 00:47:03.153
And that narrative
got put in your mind

00:47:03.153 --> 00:47:05.022
from someone in your childhood,

00:47:05.022 --> 00:47:06.290
I don't wanna go
too deep with you,

00:47:06.290 --> 00:47:09.259
but someone in your
past has said to you

00:47:09.259 --> 00:47:11.862
and made you feel at some
point like you weren't enough.

00:47:11.862 --> 00:47:15.365
And so then you start to feel
like, if I make these actions

00:47:15.365 --> 00:47:18.435
and I do these things,
then I'll be worthy.

00:47:18.435 --> 00:47:19.903
I'm good enough, I'm here.

00:47:19.903 --> 00:47:21.672
- But then when you achieve
those things of course,

00:47:21.672 --> 00:47:22.873
it's not enough.

00:47:22.873 --> 00:47:24.274
And you have to chase
that like a dragon

00:47:24.274 --> 00:47:25.642
for the rest of your life.

00:47:25.642 --> 00:47:28.245
I mean, yeah, my upbringing
was all, not that,

00:47:29.112 --> 00:47:31.148
it's like this weird thing

00:47:31.148 --> 00:47:32.983
as I feel guilty
talking about this.

00:47:32.983 --> 00:47:35.419
This is something I
explored with (indistinct).

00:47:35.419 --> 00:47:37.087
He's like, your parents
did the best they could.

00:47:37.087 --> 00:47:38.589
It's not about them
being good or bad.

00:47:38.589 --> 00:47:42.793
But I think that there was a
conditional aspect to the love

00:47:42.793 --> 00:47:46.430
in that we love you more
when you're excelling.

00:47:46.430 --> 00:47:47.898
Like it was a very education

00:47:47.898 --> 00:47:49.800
and achievement
oriented household.

00:47:49.800 --> 00:47:52.169
And I got validation
out of playing that game

00:47:52.169 --> 00:47:53.570
and succeeding at that game.

00:47:53.570 --> 00:47:57.241
And that gets cemented
into my hard wiring that

00:47:57.241 --> 00:48:02.179
today at 54, I'm still
playing out that pattern

00:48:03.046 --> 00:48:04.314
in a very dysfunctional way.

00:48:04.314 --> 00:48:05.449
- Yeah, I mean, what
you just said there

00:48:05.449 --> 00:48:07.451
about the validation

00:48:07.451 --> 00:48:08.819
in unhealthy way is
I think something

00:48:08.819 --> 00:48:11.088
that most people
experience in their homes

00:48:11.088 --> 00:48:12.589
and it goes back to
that conversation

00:48:12.589 --> 00:48:15.325
we had earlier about
schools and sort of like,

00:48:15.325 --> 00:48:18.295
the life skills we're not
receiving because you come home,

00:48:18.295 --> 00:48:21.431
you get A, everyone's cheering,
they put it on fridge.

00:48:22.866 --> 00:48:26.236
But when you say I found a
passion that makes me happy

00:48:26.236 --> 00:48:29.539
that might not tell your
parents, you can make money.

00:48:29.539 --> 00:48:31.775
They don't react the same way.

00:48:31.775 --> 00:48:34.144
I also think about this
when I was raising my kids.

00:48:34.144 --> 00:48:35.646
I had this philosophy
in my home that

00:48:35.646 --> 00:48:38.048
if you told me the truth, you
would never get in trouble.

00:48:38.048 --> 00:48:40.717
And I think we do a
disservice to children

00:48:40.717 --> 00:48:43.387
in the way that we're
grooming them of when

00:48:43.387 --> 00:48:44.988
you tell your kids to be honest.

00:48:44.988 --> 00:48:46.990
But when they're honest,
they get punished.

00:48:46.990 --> 00:48:49.526
Like what kind of
backwards way of training

00:48:49.526 --> 00:48:50.761
and teaching someone.

00:48:50.761 --> 00:48:52.229
It's similar to
like, you do good.

00:48:52.229 --> 00:48:54.898
So now you get what you want...

00:48:54.898 --> 00:48:56.366
you feel loved.

00:48:56.366 --> 00:48:58.402
It's like a backwards way that
we really have to look at,

00:48:58.402 --> 00:49:00.237
like the way that we're,

00:49:00.237 --> 00:49:01.905
what we're teaching
unconsciously

00:49:01.905 --> 00:49:03.407
and consciously to each other.

00:49:03.407 --> 00:49:05.509
And so for, in my house,
if my kids got in trouble

00:49:05.509 --> 00:49:08.078
and they told me the truth,
they didn't get punished.

00:49:08.078 --> 00:49:12.582
They got rewarded with a hug,
with love, with more support,

00:49:12.582 --> 00:49:15.552
with quality time, because,

00:49:15.552 --> 00:49:17.154
and now we have a relationship.

00:49:17.154 --> 00:49:18.655
My kids don't lie to me anymore

00:49:18.655 --> 00:49:21.992
because now they don't fear
coming and tell me the truth.

00:49:21.992 --> 00:49:25.362
And I broke this sort
of cycle in my household

00:49:25.362 --> 00:49:27.164
and I'm not saying
there's other cycles

00:49:27.164 --> 00:49:28.966
that are not still
getting perpetuated,

00:49:28.966 --> 00:49:32.769
but in my household at least
where my kids don't feel like

00:49:32.769 --> 00:49:37.774
they need to lie to get
that validation externally

00:49:38.942 --> 00:49:41.078
because they know their
truth is good enough.

00:49:41.078 --> 00:49:43.513
And you know, it's one of
those things where again

00:49:43.513 --> 00:49:44.982
if someone would told you that,

00:49:44.982 --> 00:49:47.884
hey, make an F, it doesn't
mean that you're a bad person.

00:49:47.884 --> 00:49:49.353
I'm still gonna love you.

00:49:49.353 --> 00:49:51.088
I'm still gonna celebrate
you as a human being.

00:49:51.088 --> 00:49:52.589
What would have that
have done for you?

00:49:52.589 --> 00:49:56.159
Like as a child, that would
have been amazing for you.

00:49:56.159 --> 00:49:57.661
- Yeah.

00:49:57.661 --> 00:50:00.797
But bridging that gap, like
overcoming that patterning

00:50:02.866 --> 00:50:05.569
intellectually versus
emotionally is a journey, right.

00:50:05.569 --> 00:50:09.206
Like you can say, look,
if you tell me the truth

00:50:09.206 --> 00:50:10.273
I'm not gonna get mad at you.

00:50:10.273 --> 00:50:11.341
And then you're like, you're mad

00:50:11.341 --> 00:50:13.043
but you're trying not to act

00:50:13.043 --> 00:50:14.344
as you know it's
the wrong thing.

00:50:14.344 --> 00:50:16.346
That's very different from,

00:50:16.346 --> 00:50:19.349
really coming with a
genuine, authentic,

00:50:19.349 --> 00:50:21.885
sense of compassion
into that dynamic.

00:50:21.885 --> 00:50:23.387
- Oh, completely.

00:50:23.387 --> 00:50:25.455
- Well, that's where, like
I said before the rewarding,

00:50:25.455 --> 00:50:27.157
understanding what the
reward is gonna be,

00:50:27.157 --> 00:50:28.625
understanding what
your intention.

00:50:28.625 --> 00:50:31.828
So the word reward for me is
also synonymous with intention.

00:50:31.828 --> 00:50:33.997
So my intention here
is through my actions

00:50:33.997 --> 00:50:35.332
to show you that I love you,

00:50:35.332 --> 00:50:37.367
to give you this reward of like,

00:50:37.367 --> 00:50:39.302
yes, I feel something inside

00:50:39.302 --> 00:50:42.439
but externally you're going
to only be embraced by love.

00:50:42.439 --> 00:50:44.574
You're only gonna be
embraced by positivity.

00:50:44.574 --> 00:50:46.309
And it does take time.

00:50:46.309 --> 00:50:47.744
It does take effort.

00:50:49.446 --> 00:50:51.248
And this is where
training does help me.

00:50:51.248 --> 00:50:53.150
Because as a parent, it
helped me to realize like

00:50:53.150 --> 00:50:55.719
this is not gonna be healthy
if I do something opposite.

00:50:55.719 --> 00:50:59.056
But, it does take time,
but everyone can get there.

00:50:59.056 --> 00:51:00.357
We forget that we,

00:51:00.357 --> 00:51:02.192
all it takes for you to
change your self-esteem

00:51:02.192 --> 00:51:03.460
or the way that you're living

00:51:03.460 --> 00:51:06.163
is to practice the
same thing daily.

00:51:06.163 --> 00:51:07.697
That's all you have to do.

00:51:07.697 --> 00:51:09.332
If you get up in the morning
time, you look in the mirror

00:51:09.332 --> 00:51:11.868
or the first thing you do
is you downplay yourself

00:51:11.868 --> 00:51:13.503
and say, oh, look
at how I look here.

00:51:13.503 --> 00:51:16.106
I'll look at my nose, look
at my hair, it's going down.

00:51:16.106 --> 00:51:18.608
You're practicing everyday
knocking your self-esteem down.

00:51:18.608 --> 00:51:20.944
If you practicing in the
morning time coming in

00:51:20.944 --> 00:51:22.312
and saying to your kids,

00:51:22.312 --> 00:51:25.248
hey, are you ready
for school, whatever,

00:51:25.248 --> 00:51:26.716
that first statement is,

00:51:26.716 --> 00:51:28.885
you're practicing like
what is most important

00:51:28.885 --> 00:51:30.353
and what is most valuable to you

00:51:30.353 --> 00:51:32.089
versus coming in the room,

00:51:32.089 --> 00:51:33.590
like I used to do
this with my kids

00:51:33.590 --> 00:51:34.791
where I would come in the
room in the morning time

00:51:34.791 --> 00:51:36.593
and before I would
wake them up and say,

00:51:36.593 --> 00:51:37.928
we got to get to work.

00:51:37.928 --> 00:51:39.162
I would give them a kiss

00:51:39.162 --> 00:51:40.664
and just be like,
did you sleep well?

00:51:40.664 --> 00:51:42.632
And I would sit with them
for like 10, 15 minutes

00:51:42.632 --> 00:51:44.835
to understand that
my value is not

00:51:44.835 --> 00:51:46.603
how fast you're gonna
get ready for school.

00:51:46.603 --> 00:51:48.438
Not saying that's not a value,

00:51:48.438 --> 00:51:50.574
but that's not the top
of my priority list.

00:51:50.574 --> 00:51:53.176
And I think that we can
all learn how to practice,

00:51:53.176 --> 00:51:54.478
what's our intention,

00:51:54.478 --> 00:51:57.714
what's our values
more verses making

00:51:57.714 --> 00:51:59.749
these (indistinct)
reaction of, I'm busy.

00:51:59.749 --> 00:52:01.418
What happens when
you walk in the door,

00:52:01.418 --> 00:52:02.752
do you go straight to the TV?

00:52:02.752 --> 00:52:04.054
Do you go straight to your phone

00:52:04.054 --> 00:52:05.822
or do you take a
moment to connect?

00:52:05.822 --> 00:52:08.225
And if you practice
day by day doing that

00:52:08.225 --> 00:52:09.493
then things get better.

00:52:11.561 --> 00:52:13.563
- What's the current
blind spot with you?

00:52:13.563 --> 00:52:15.265
Like what's the thing
that trips you up

00:52:15.265 --> 00:52:17.634
and causes the struggle?
- With me.

00:52:17.634 --> 00:52:18.468
- Yeah.

00:52:19.436 --> 00:52:20.670
- Ooh.

00:52:20.670 --> 00:52:22.272
I mean, right now it's,
I guess a lot of like,

00:52:23.206 --> 00:52:24.141
cause I'm out of
one relationship

00:52:24.141 --> 00:52:25.408
that I was in for 10 years and.

00:52:25.408 --> 00:52:27.344
- And you were engaged
for a long time.

00:52:27.344 --> 00:52:28.845
- Yeah, we were engaged
for a year and a half.

00:52:28.845 --> 00:52:31.548
And so it's a lot of
like, understanding like

00:52:31.548 --> 00:52:33.450
the things that I
didn't even know

00:52:34.618 --> 00:52:36.353
were lurking I need
the surface that.

00:52:36.353 --> 00:52:37.654
I guess I didn't heal from

00:52:37.654 --> 00:52:39.156
or like were bothering me

00:52:39.156 --> 00:52:41.758
and like how that can just
come out in a second now

00:52:41.758 --> 00:52:42.959
and like being like,

00:52:42.959 --> 00:52:44.127
oh, I need to really
go back through

00:52:44.127 --> 00:52:46.763
and like really evaluate
what was happening

00:52:46.763 --> 00:52:49.232
in this relationship.

00:52:49.232 --> 00:52:52.402
And really being truthful
with myself about like things

00:52:52.402 --> 00:52:53.703
and how they made me feel.

00:52:53.703 --> 00:52:54.938
And so I think that's the thing

00:52:54.938 --> 00:52:58.475
that's tripping me
up right now of like,

00:52:58.475 --> 00:53:00.510
you think that you have a
handle on a relationship

00:53:00.510 --> 00:53:02.345
but you realize that every
relationship is different

00:53:02.345 --> 00:53:03.780
and every relationship needs

00:53:03.780 --> 00:53:07.250
to be evaluated
differently and equally.

00:53:07.250 --> 00:53:08.919
And I think that's
such an important thing

00:53:08.919 --> 00:53:10.453
because I got out of a
relationship thinking

00:53:10.453 --> 00:53:12.022
I'm fine, everything's good.

00:53:12.022 --> 00:53:14.391
We ended on unhealthy place.

00:53:14.391 --> 00:53:15.525
And then I was like,

00:53:15.525 --> 00:53:17.894
oh no, I'm not fine, I'm not.

00:53:17.894 --> 00:53:19.829
There was things that
were lurking underneath

00:53:19.829 --> 00:53:21.731
that weren't the
big traumatic things

00:53:21.731 --> 00:53:23.667
but they were still there,

00:53:23.667 --> 00:53:25.802
and like finding that balance.

00:53:25.802 --> 00:53:27.204
- And even under the
best circumstances,

00:53:27.204 --> 00:53:29.339
when you've been with
somebody for a very long time

00:53:29.339 --> 00:53:31.274
and then suddenly you're not.

00:53:31.274 --> 00:53:32.809
If you think you're not,

00:53:32.809 --> 00:53:34.711
dealing with some kind of,

00:53:34.711 --> 00:53:38.848
latent emotional issue, you're
in complete denial, right.

00:53:38.848 --> 00:53:41.651
- Yeah, completely, yeah.

00:53:41.651 --> 00:53:43.453
And I say, this, it's
not regards to me

00:53:43.453 --> 00:53:45.755
but I just wanna give
your listeners a thing.

00:53:45.755 --> 00:53:48.191
It's okay to tell people
don't rush my healing.

00:53:48.191 --> 00:53:51.094
I think some times we feel
as if like we're grieving

00:53:51.094 --> 00:53:52.429
and we're going
through something,

00:53:52.429 --> 00:53:54.764
whether it's like the
actual physical death

00:53:54.764 --> 00:53:57.167
of someone actually the
end of a relationship,

00:53:57.167 --> 00:54:00.103
or even just grieving
the financial security

00:54:00.103 --> 00:54:02.038
you thought you were gonna
have, whatever you're grieving,

00:54:02.038 --> 00:54:03.573
and especially in
this COVID world.

00:54:03.573 --> 00:54:04.975
I think sometimes,

00:54:04.975 --> 00:54:07.077
we put a timeline on
other people's grief

00:54:07.077 --> 00:54:09.980
and other people's way
of healing and it's okay

00:54:09.980 --> 00:54:11.781
to tell people don't
put a timeline on it.

00:54:11.781 --> 00:54:14.784
Like, I don't like
or appreciate,

00:54:14.784 --> 00:54:16.786
like when I hear friends saying,

00:54:16.786 --> 00:54:20.056
oh my gosh, you're
still not over that guy.

00:54:20.056 --> 00:54:23.193
It's like, just because
you've gotten over it,

00:54:23.193 --> 00:54:25.729
doesn't mean you
can rush my healing.

00:54:25.729 --> 00:54:29.366
A better response is, what are
ways that I can support you?

00:54:29.366 --> 00:54:30.734
How can I get you
the help you can get

00:54:30.734 --> 00:54:32.936
you need so that you can
feel like you can heal,

00:54:32.936 --> 00:54:34.804
so that you can go through
this grieving process.

00:54:34.804 --> 00:54:36.006
But I think as a culture,

00:54:36.006 --> 00:54:37.607
we rush people's
breathing too much.

00:54:37.607 --> 00:54:39.943
And I just wanna tell
people that it's okay

00:54:39.943 --> 00:54:43.079
to tell someone, you don't
rush my process of healing.

00:54:43.079 --> 00:54:44.014
- Yeah, that's beautiful.

00:54:44.014 --> 00:54:45.448
I think that's sure important.

00:54:45.448 --> 00:54:48.318
I think it's equally
applicable to,

00:54:49.886 --> 00:54:53.356
the people in your lives that
you create expectations around

00:54:53.356 --> 00:54:55.392
where they should be
with where you're at.

00:54:55.392 --> 00:54:56.960
Like whether it's your dad,

00:54:56.960 --> 00:54:59.062
and how he feels about how
you're living your life.

00:54:59.062 --> 00:55:00.497
- Oh my gosh.

00:55:00.497 --> 00:55:03.099
- We all hear those
stories of the newly sober

00:55:03.099 --> 00:55:05.769
(indistinct) 30
days and he's angry

00:55:05.769 --> 00:55:08.238
that no one's embracing him
back into their lives after

00:55:08.238 --> 00:55:10.173
he's torched everyone for years.

00:55:11.541 --> 00:55:13.576
That's a different kind of
healing and you gotta be patient

00:55:13.576 --> 00:55:16.012
with the people that you love
and that are in your circle.

00:55:16.012 --> 00:55:18.515
Allow them to have their
own their own journey

00:55:18.515 --> 00:55:20.016
with whatever it is
that they're undergoing.

00:55:20.016 --> 00:55:21.651
- Yeah, you let them
have their journey.

00:55:21.651 --> 00:55:23.153
I think, you know,

00:55:23.153 --> 00:55:24.387
that's some of the things
like when I tell people,

00:55:24.387 --> 00:55:26.022
there's two things I
wanna say that because

00:55:26.022 --> 00:55:27.490
the first is you got
to let other people

00:55:27.490 --> 00:55:28.491
have that healing, that journey.

00:55:28.491 --> 00:55:30.093
And I think it's,

00:55:30.093 --> 00:55:31.761
that's why I say
it's so important for
people to know that

00:55:31.761 --> 00:55:33.863
and to understand that
it's a dual thing,

00:55:33.863 --> 00:55:34.931
it's both sides.

00:55:35.799 --> 00:55:37.300
Because I always tell people,

00:55:37.300 --> 00:55:39.569
when I let people into my
life regarding my sexuality

00:55:39.569 --> 00:55:40.837
I don't use the term coming out,

00:55:40.837 --> 00:55:42.405
I think it give the
power to the person.

00:55:42.405 --> 00:55:43.573
- I think you said that right.

00:55:43.573 --> 00:55:44.708
Explain that a little bit.

00:55:44.708 --> 00:55:47.010
- Well, when you say coming out,

00:55:47.010 --> 00:55:48.611
it gives the power
to the other person

00:55:48.611 --> 00:55:49.979
to accept or deny you.

00:55:49.979 --> 00:55:52.582
And the process that I
actually did was let people in.

00:55:52.582 --> 00:55:54.384
And when you let someone in,

00:55:54.384 --> 00:55:57.287
you have the power to
deny or love yourself,

00:55:57.287 --> 00:55:58.488
accept or deny yourself.

00:55:58.488 --> 00:56:00.156
And so it has nothing
to do with you.

00:56:00.156 --> 00:56:01.658
It's about I'm
letting you in and

00:56:01.658 --> 00:56:03.993
if you don't wanna
be here with me,

00:56:03.993 --> 00:56:06.062
then that's fine
because I still love me.

00:56:06.062 --> 00:56:08.298
So if you've done
the work on yourself.

00:56:08.298 --> 00:56:09.566
I always think about it as,

00:56:09.566 --> 00:56:11.000
your body's a temple.

00:56:11.000 --> 00:56:13.470
So if your letting someone
into your sexuality

00:56:13.470 --> 00:56:15.004
into some part of your life,

00:56:15.004 --> 00:56:16.840
if you open that front
door and you say,

00:56:16.840 --> 00:56:18.775
come on in and they
say, I don't want to.

00:56:18.775 --> 00:56:20.944
If you've done the
work on your home,

00:56:20.944 --> 00:56:22.879
you close that door and
you walk in your own home

00:56:22.879 --> 00:56:24.914
and feel comfortable
and you feel loved.

00:56:24.914 --> 00:56:28.151
And so I think it gives
the person back the power

00:56:28.151 --> 00:56:29.652
and the understanding to,

00:56:29.652 --> 00:56:31.621
that it's not about them
accepting, denying you.

00:56:31.621 --> 00:56:33.423
It's about you
loving yourself more.

00:56:33.423 --> 00:56:35.725
And also this whole
antiquated idea

00:56:35.725 --> 00:56:37.560
of like coming out the closet.

00:56:37.560 --> 00:56:39.162
I don't know where
hell this closet is.

00:56:39.162 --> 00:56:41.664
And if there is a
closet, I wanna find it.

00:56:41.664 --> 00:56:42.932
There's gotta be a pot of gold.

00:56:42.932 --> 00:56:44.100
And if all the BS
I've been through,

00:56:44.100 --> 00:56:45.969
and I wanna burn
down this closet

00:56:45.969 --> 00:56:47.203
so nobody else has
to come out of it.

00:56:47.203 --> 00:56:49.472
Like, it's all about
letting people in.

00:56:49.472 --> 00:56:52.609
But, when I was letting
people and I would say,

00:56:52.609 --> 00:56:56.613
I understand that in my
mind, I had been dealing

00:56:56.613 --> 00:57:00.483
with an understanding and
loving my sexuality and who I am

00:57:00.483 --> 00:57:04.154
for five years before
I let anyone know.

00:57:04.154 --> 00:57:05.822
So when I let
certain people know,

00:57:05.822 --> 00:57:09.726
I realized they had to
grieve an entire identity

00:57:09.726 --> 00:57:12.028
that they have constructed
in there minds.

00:57:12.028 --> 00:57:14.497
And I'm not saying that
it's my fault or that

00:57:14.497 --> 00:57:16.332
they have any blame
or I have any blame.

00:57:16.332 --> 00:57:17.801
But you have to understand that

00:57:17.801 --> 00:57:20.270
if this is what I've
known about you,

00:57:20.270 --> 00:57:22.705
and one day you say,
nope, erase that

00:57:22.705 --> 00:57:24.374
this is something different.

00:57:24.374 --> 00:57:25.842
Yes, you want them
to get to a point

00:57:25.842 --> 00:57:28.044
where they can accept you
and love you for who you are,

00:57:28.044 --> 00:57:29.946
but they have to have
their time to grieve.

00:57:29.946 --> 00:57:32.348
And so I think you have to
give people their time to heal.

00:57:32.348 --> 00:57:33.883
So like when I worked
in social services

00:57:33.883 --> 00:57:36.453
and I worked with kids, I
would say understand that

00:57:36.453 --> 00:57:38.388
it took you five
years in your mind

00:57:38.388 --> 00:57:41.124
and talking to other little
friends to get to where you are.

00:57:41.124 --> 00:57:43.460
It might take your
mom or your sister,

00:57:43.460 --> 00:57:45.128
your cousin, five years too.

00:57:45.128 --> 00:57:47.497
We all would love the beautiful
stories that we see on TV

00:57:47.497 --> 00:57:48.965
where people just are like,

00:57:48.965 --> 00:57:51.000
I accept you and I love you
right here off the spot.

00:57:51.000 --> 00:57:52.635
But most of the time
when that happens

00:57:52.635 --> 00:57:55.305
it's because that person
had some type of inclination

00:57:55.305 --> 00:57:58.374
and they were already doing
the healing on their own

00:57:58.374 --> 00:58:00.243
or they had a
friend in their past

00:58:00.243 --> 00:58:01.544
that they were
healing on their own

00:58:01.544 --> 00:58:04.914
and healing their biases
and everything else.

00:58:04.914 --> 00:58:06.583
But you have to take that time

00:58:06.583 --> 00:58:08.084
to let other people
heal as well.

00:58:08.084 --> 00:58:10.386
And so it's okay
for a parent to say,

00:58:10.386 --> 00:58:12.288
I need a little time to
grieve and to heal myself.

00:58:12.288 --> 00:58:13.823
It doesn't mean
I don't love you.

00:58:13.823 --> 00:58:15.091
I love you.

00:58:15.091 --> 00:58:16.993
I need the time to
process and heal.

00:58:16.993 --> 00:58:18.461
But secondly, on
what you were saying,

00:58:18.461 --> 00:58:20.230
when you're talking about
the sobriety and like the guy

00:58:20.230 --> 00:58:21.598
who was like 30 (indistinct).

00:58:21.598 --> 00:58:22.799
I think there's something
else that we do.

00:58:22.799 --> 00:58:24.667
And this is something
I say all the time

00:58:24.667 --> 00:58:26.970
which is compression
is the thief of joy.

00:58:26.970 --> 00:58:28.838
When you compare yourself
and your experiences

00:58:28.838 --> 00:58:31.174
to other people, you're
still your own joy.

00:58:31.174 --> 00:58:34.844
And I think as a culture
we do that way too often.

00:58:34.844 --> 00:58:38.081
We look at people on Instagram
and instead of thinking about

00:58:38.081 --> 00:58:40.149
what we have in our own
lives and how beautiful

00:58:40.149 --> 00:58:42.318
the small and big
things that we have,

00:58:42.318 --> 00:58:44.721
we end up comparing
our bodies, our minds,

00:58:44.721 --> 00:58:47.724
our inner circles,

00:58:47.724 --> 00:58:51.394
little trinkets that you have
to what other people have.

00:58:51.394 --> 00:58:53.129
And you have to stop doing that.

00:58:53.129 --> 00:58:54.597
If you have 30 days,

00:58:54.597 --> 00:58:57.367
don't compare yourself
to what other people are,

00:58:57.367 --> 00:58:58.902
what they're not doing.

00:58:58.902 --> 00:59:00.103
They're not accepting you.

00:59:00.103 --> 00:59:02.539
Again, they're on their
journey of healing.

00:59:02.539 --> 00:59:05.141
But just don't compare
yourself to anything else.

00:59:05.141 --> 00:59:07.243
Just know it will
happen in your own time.

00:59:07.243 --> 00:59:09.979
Don't steal your own joy
focusing on something else.

00:59:09.979 --> 00:59:14.551
- You can also steal that
joy by comparing yourself to

00:59:14.551 --> 00:59:16.119
an idealized version of yourself

00:59:16.119 --> 00:59:17.954
or past version of yourself.

00:59:17.954 --> 00:59:20.123
So you don't actually even
need that other person.

00:59:20.123 --> 00:59:21.291
- You don't need it.

00:59:21.291 --> 00:59:22.225
You can just looking
at your own--

00:59:22.225 --> 00:59:23.192
- I do, it's all in my head.

00:59:23.192 --> 00:59:24.360
- Oh my gosh.

00:59:24.360 --> 00:59:26.329
That's such a
great point because

00:59:26.329 --> 00:59:28.331
I have a girlfriend
who right now is

00:59:29.465 --> 00:59:30.867
on a physical
health journey where

00:59:30.867 --> 00:59:32.468
her body has changed
after children.

00:59:32.468 --> 00:59:34.537
And she constantly it's like,

00:59:34.537 --> 00:59:36.940
oh, my old body,
I want that body.

00:59:36.940 --> 00:59:41.945
And I'm like, just the biology,

00:59:43.079 --> 00:59:44.013
none of our bodies
will ever be the same

00:59:44.013 --> 00:59:45.448
as they were from day one.

00:59:45.448 --> 00:59:47.984
And so you have to
be okay and except

00:59:47.984 --> 00:59:49.986
that your body where
it is now is enough.

00:59:49.986 --> 00:59:52.989
And if you wanna work
out to be healthier,

00:59:52.989 --> 00:59:55.592
but you have to stop comparing
yourself to that old body,

00:59:55.592 --> 00:59:58.461
like you said because
you're now stealing the joy

00:59:58.461 --> 01:00:00.597
of being like, there's
something beautiful

01:00:00.597 --> 01:00:02.065
about whatever
body you're in now.

01:00:02.065 --> 01:00:03.833
There's something great about it

01:00:03.833 --> 01:00:05.401
and find what that is.

01:00:05.401 --> 01:00:08.104
Again, going back to
practicing in the mirror,

01:00:08.104 --> 01:00:10.206
what is one thing on this
new body that you can love

01:00:10.206 --> 01:00:13.142
about yourself versus comparing
yourself to an old body.

01:00:13.142 --> 01:00:15.445
Stop stealing your own joy by
comparing yourself to others

01:00:15.445 --> 01:00:18.381
or your old version of yourself,
I think it's so critical.

01:00:18.381 --> 01:00:19.616
I'm glad you had that piece.

01:00:19.616 --> 01:00:21.718
- In addition to
that more broadly,

01:00:21.718 --> 01:00:24.721
like outside of the context of,

01:00:24.721 --> 01:00:28.391
sharing some aspect of who
you are and being attached

01:00:28.391 --> 01:00:30.860
to how people are
going to receive that,

01:00:30.860 --> 01:00:34.597
the growth is in
understanding that

01:00:34.597 --> 01:00:37.634
how people feel about you on
some fundamental level really

01:00:37.634 --> 01:00:38.534
isn't your business.

01:00:38.534 --> 01:00:40.036
- At all.

01:00:40.036 --> 01:00:42.972
- And even if it was, you're
unable to control that.

01:00:42.972 --> 01:00:44.507
- [Karamo] Completely.

01:00:44.507 --> 01:00:47.377
- And the extent to which
human beings try to do that

01:00:47.377 --> 01:00:51.681
I think is really the engine
of so much unhappiness.

01:00:51.681 --> 01:00:55.585
Like we're all trying to
control our environments,

01:00:55.585 --> 01:00:58.521
position ourselves to be
received in a certain way.

01:00:58.521 --> 01:01:02.458
And it's all this delusional
attempt to manipulate the world

01:01:02.458 --> 01:01:03.826
so that it,

01:01:03.826 --> 01:01:06.295
appears as we would
like it to be.

01:01:06.295 --> 01:01:08.898
And the freedom only
comes in the surrender

01:01:08.898 --> 01:01:12.301
to the idea that that is
beyond our ability to control

01:01:12.301 --> 01:01:14.003
and to make peace with that,

01:01:14.003 --> 01:01:17.140
to accept the fact that
that's them, I'm me.

01:01:17.140 --> 01:01:20.243
I'm only in charge of myself,
how I feel about myself,

01:01:20.243 --> 01:01:23.246
my actions, my
reactions, my thoughts.

01:01:24.681 --> 01:01:26.015
- How did you go to that place
where you have that freedom?

01:01:26.015 --> 01:01:27.417
- I'm still working on it.

01:01:27.417 --> 01:01:28.718
(Karamo laughs)

01:01:28.718 --> 01:01:29.752
You know what I mean.

01:01:29.752 --> 01:01:30.953
I mean, you know,

01:01:30.953 --> 01:01:33.823
I got sober in 98
and spent a long time

01:01:33.823 --> 01:01:35.825
in a treatment center and,

01:01:35.825 --> 01:01:38.661
very much steeped in the
recovery community here in LA.

01:01:38.661 --> 01:01:42.732
And I do therapy and
I have my wife who is,

01:01:42.732 --> 01:01:47.036
been instrumental in helping
guide me through a lot of--

01:01:47.036 --> 01:01:48.705
- She's pretty pretty
amazing, we met briefly.

01:01:48.705 --> 01:01:51.374
- Identifying my
shortcomings and,

01:01:51.374 --> 01:01:55.344
just being, I don't mean
that in a negative way.

01:01:55.344 --> 01:01:59.248
I mean that in a way of being
able to see what I can't see

01:01:59.248 --> 01:02:01.284
and being able to communicate,

01:02:01.284 --> 01:02:02.752
hey, you might
wanna look at this,

01:02:02.752 --> 01:02:04.087
like, you're doing this thing,

01:02:04.087 --> 01:02:05.354
like maybe think about that

01:02:05.354 --> 01:02:06.989
or is there a better
way to do that.

01:02:06.989 --> 01:02:08.224
- Completely.

01:02:08.224 --> 01:02:10.293
- But you know, I'm the
furthest from perfect.

01:02:10.293 --> 01:02:13.896
And it's a strange situation,
like in hosting this podcast

01:02:13.896 --> 01:02:15.298
and people say like,
how do you do this?

01:02:15.298 --> 01:02:16.499
How do you that?

01:02:16.499 --> 01:02:18.301
I'm like, dude,
like, I'm trying to,

01:02:18.301 --> 01:02:19.368
why do you think I'm doing this?

01:02:19.368 --> 01:02:20.770
I'm trying to figure this out.

01:02:20.770 --> 01:02:22.271
- We figure this out together.

01:02:22.271 --> 01:02:25.041
- Like I don't stand on any
kind of pedestal in terms of,

01:02:25.041 --> 01:02:27.276
knowing answers that
other people don't.

01:02:27.276 --> 01:02:30.613
Like I grapple with this stuff
mightily every single day.

01:02:30.613 --> 01:02:32.849
- Yeah, I gotta tell
you, I'm the same way.

01:02:32.849 --> 01:02:34.150
I'm the same way,

01:02:34.150 --> 01:02:36.352
like I shared with you earlier,

01:02:36.352 --> 01:02:38.721
when it comes to
myself, sometimes I,

01:02:38.721 --> 01:02:42.158
of course I'm able to
especially identify,

01:02:42.158 --> 01:02:44.527
someone's trauma or
triggers and help them

01:02:44.527 --> 01:02:45.728
to kind of navigate
through that.

01:02:45.728 --> 01:02:46.996
But, you know,

01:02:46.996 --> 01:02:48.464
I'm still learning
constantly myself,

01:02:48.464 --> 01:02:49.766
we're all human beings.

01:02:49.766 --> 01:02:51.734
And I think that
freedom of letting go

01:02:51.734 --> 01:02:53.703
is something that
we all strive to.

01:02:53.703 --> 01:02:55.471
And I feel like I get
closer on some days

01:02:55.471 --> 01:02:56.439
and other days I'm like,

01:02:56.439 --> 01:02:58.808
damn, I missed the boat today.

01:02:58.808 --> 01:02:59.976
I just really missed it.

01:02:59.976 --> 01:03:03.279
But it's also, giving myself,

01:03:03.279 --> 01:03:04.347
you know, forgiving myself.

01:03:04.347 --> 01:03:05.882
I'm big on that.

01:03:05.882 --> 01:03:10.453
Like I once a day will say
to myself, I forgive you.

01:03:10.453 --> 01:03:12.255
I literally would do
it in the morning,

01:03:12.255 --> 01:03:14.123
sometimes I'll do
it in the night.

01:03:14.123 --> 01:03:15.358
I'll just look at myself in
the mirror and I'd be like,

01:03:15.358 --> 01:03:16.425
I forgive you.

01:03:16.425 --> 01:03:18.995
And it's my way of saying, like,

01:03:18.995 --> 01:03:19.929
you're human.

01:03:19.929 --> 01:03:21.164
You made that mistake today,

01:03:21.164 --> 01:03:23.599
but what are you
gonna do tomorrow?

01:03:23.599 --> 01:03:25.368
How are you gonna make a
better choice tomorrow.

01:03:25.368 --> 01:03:27.970
And I think sometimes
people are scared to make

01:03:27.970 --> 01:03:30.072
the better choice
tomorrow because

01:03:30.072 --> 01:03:32.608
they hadn't forgiven themselves
for what they did yesterday.

01:03:32.608 --> 01:03:34.410
And, you know,

01:03:34.410 --> 01:03:38.080
when you can let go of your
ego and say I forgive myself,

01:03:38.080 --> 01:03:40.616
now let me go and see if I can,

01:03:40.616 --> 01:03:43.152
rebuild the bridge that I,

01:03:43.152 --> 01:03:45.254
broke with someone else by,

01:03:45.254 --> 01:03:47.957
acknowledging with through
not being defensive,

01:03:47.957 --> 01:03:49.325
what I've done.

01:03:49.325 --> 01:03:52.028
I tried to start
conversations really,

01:03:52.028 --> 01:03:54.897
quickly with like,
listen, I know what I did.

01:03:54.897 --> 01:03:56.933
I mean, let me be very clear.

01:03:56.933 --> 01:03:59.535
Like we don't have to spend
two hours of like pretending.

01:03:59.535 --> 01:04:01.137
I know what I did,
and I'm sorry.

01:04:01.137 --> 01:04:04.073
And I think forgiving
myself first of the actions

01:04:04.073 --> 01:04:05.875
and then saying,
will you forgive me

01:04:05.875 --> 01:04:08.110
and allowing someone to be
on their journey of saying,

01:04:08.110 --> 01:04:10.613
yes or no, I might
need some time is okay.

01:04:10.613 --> 01:04:12.715
And I think that's how I
get to that freedom as well.

01:04:12.715 --> 01:04:15.218
- Yeah, by doing
your own inventory

01:04:15.218 --> 01:04:19.889
and then leading with honesty,
integrity and vulnerability.

01:04:19.889 --> 01:04:22.758
Like when you initiate those
challenging conversations

01:04:22.758 --> 01:04:25.828
from a perspective of,
let me just own up front

01:04:25.828 --> 01:04:29.398
like my side of this
equation that went sideways

01:04:29.398 --> 01:04:31.801
you're in a much better
situation in terms

01:04:31.801 --> 01:04:33.703
of being able to navigate
to a healthy place.

01:04:33.703 --> 01:04:35.304
- And also take some defense.

01:04:35.304 --> 01:04:37.106
I'm telling anybody who's
listening, just should try it.

01:04:37.106 --> 01:04:39.308
Like if you're in a
situation with somebody,

01:04:39.308 --> 01:04:41.878
we all know at our core
what role we played

01:04:41.878 --> 01:04:45.648
for the most part and the demise
of relationships, intimate

01:04:45.648 --> 01:04:47.650
or personal or friendships.

01:04:47.650 --> 01:04:49.318
You know what you did,

01:04:49.318 --> 01:04:50.820
you might not wanna acknowledge.

01:04:50.820 --> 01:04:52.355
You might not wanna say it
out loud, you might not.

01:04:52.355 --> 01:04:53.289
But you know.

01:04:53.289 --> 01:04:54.824
I'm telling you next time

01:04:54.824 --> 01:04:56.726
you're in a space with that
person, just acknowledge it.

01:04:56.726 --> 01:04:58.794
Just say it, like
just go for it.

01:04:58.794 --> 01:05:00.563
I'm telling you
it's going to create

01:05:01.964 --> 01:05:04.100
a healthier relationship
like you can't even imagine.

01:05:04.100 --> 01:05:06.135
Just being like, it's
just surrendering.

01:05:06.135 --> 01:05:07.203
It's where like, I get it.

01:05:07.203 --> 01:05:08.671
I did it, I'm sorry.

01:05:08.671 --> 01:05:11.574
Like, I'm not gonna
defend it right now.

01:05:11.574 --> 01:05:12.742
I did it,

01:05:12.742 --> 01:05:15.244
and we can have a
conversation later about

01:05:15.244 --> 01:05:18.014
why I might have done it
but I'm not gonna defend it.

01:05:18.014 --> 01:05:19.582
I just did it, sorry.

01:05:19.582 --> 01:05:21.450
- That's the mistake I
think a lot of people make.

01:05:21.450 --> 01:05:24.387
Like, they'll do that but then
there's always a but or...

01:05:24.387 --> 01:05:25.354
- Don't do the but.

01:05:25.354 --> 01:05:26.989
Just leave the buts out.

01:05:26.989 --> 01:05:28.391
- Get rid of the but.

01:05:28.391 --> 01:05:30.459
Yeah, like let it
land without that

01:05:30.459 --> 01:05:32.295
is always much better.

01:05:32.295 --> 01:05:37.300
Well, I think like
your true gift is that

01:05:39.001 --> 01:05:43.873
you carry this vibration and
you do it in a masculine body

01:05:43.873 --> 01:05:46.242
and you enter into
these scenarios

01:05:46.242 --> 01:05:48.878
with these various
individuals on the TV show,

01:05:48.878 --> 01:05:51.347
you help them see
their way through

01:05:51.347 --> 01:05:54.517
their own kind of mental
and emotional hurdles.

01:05:54.517 --> 01:05:56.085
But in a broader sense,

01:05:56.085 --> 01:06:00.089
you're giving men
permission to grapple with

01:06:00.089 --> 01:06:03.492
and confront their emotions
in a culture in which,

01:06:03.492 --> 01:06:05.928
we've come a long way,
but let's face it.

01:06:05.928 --> 01:06:07.596
Like there's still
a long way to go

01:06:07.596 --> 01:06:11.233
in terms of getting the average
dude to think about things

01:06:11.233 --> 01:06:15.838
like vulnerability and
inventory and loving oneself

01:06:15.838 --> 01:06:18.674
and even talking
about their fears.

01:06:18.674 --> 01:06:19.475
- Yeah.

01:06:21.043 --> 01:06:24.847
I don't know where in our
culture, we went wrong.

01:06:24.847 --> 01:06:26.949
I don't know where,
I don't know when.

01:06:26.949 --> 01:06:31.187
But like this idea that
men aren't vulnerable

01:06:31.187 --> 01:06:33.689
that they don't hurt,
that they don't have.

01:06:35.624 --> 01:06:38.494
Well, actually I do have an
idea of where this shit started.

01:06:38.494 --> 01:06:39.962
Like for me, I believe,

01:06:39.962 --> 01:06:41.697
I believe there is a God

01:06:41.697 --> 01:06:44.700
but I think it's narrow-minded
to believe that God is a man.

01:06:44.700 --> 01:06:47.436
When we see everything on
this earth, it's created

01:06:47.436 --> 01:06:48.537
and formed by women.

01:06:49.405 --> 01:06:52.174
I'm like now he's,

01:06:52.174 --> 01:06:53.976
a guy all of a sudden did this.

01:06:53.976 --> 01:06:57.580
And so, I revere women because
the people who are usually

01:06:57.580 --> 01:06:59.782
my spiritual guys, my mentors
have always been women.

01:06:59.782 --> 01:07:02.885
So I think that women are
the most powerful beings

01:07:02.885 --> 01:07:04.387
on this face of this earth.

01:07:04.387 --> 01:07:07.957
And I think that when we sort
of put women into a category

01:07:07.957 --> 01:07:11.994
because we were intimidated or
afraid of their awesome power

01:07:11.994 --> 01:07:15.364
is when men start to feel
this sort of like need to say,

01:07:16.766 --> 01:07:20.703
well, okay, I'm stronger
now because I'm limiting you

01:07:20.703 --> 01:07:23.806
and I'm gonna have this
power and say that look

01:07:23.806 --> 01:07:27.443
like I'm strong, but because
of your wholeness, you cry.

01:07:27.443 --> 01:07:28.277
I don't cry.

01:07:28.277 --> 01:07:29.945
I've diminished that.

01:07:29.945 --> 01:07:32.381
And I think we did
it to ourselves.

01:07:32.381 --> 01:07:34.483
And then I think through time,

01:07:34.483 --> 01:07:37.987
it got perpetuated through
movies, stereotypes,

01:07:37.987 --> 01:07:39.889
and then even some women
started subscribe to it.

01:07:39.889 --> 01:07:42.525
Like my man is not strong
if he shows emotion

01:07:42.525 --> 01:07:44.026
or something of that nature.

01:07:44.026 --> 01:07:46.562
But I think what's happening
now is we're seeing that shift

01:07:46.562 --> 01:07:49.698
because of the shift of our
culture with mental health

01:07:49.698 --> 01:07:52.535
this period of like,
we can't keep on going

01:07:52.535 --> 01:07:53.803
and ignoring this.

01:07:53.803 --> 01:07:55.604
We saw what happened
to our grandparents

01:07:55.604 --> 01:07:56.806
and our great-grandparents.

01:07:56.806 --> 01:07:58.407
And we're seeing what happens

01:07:58.407 --> 01:08:00.676
when generations completely
ignore their mental health

01:08:00.676 --> 01:08:02.144
and only focused on
their physical health.

01:08:02.144 --> 01:08:04.447
And I think men are
starting to wake up to, like

01:08:04.447 --> 01:08:07.750
I have to be better for myself.

01:08:07.750 --> 01:08:09.919
I have to be better for
the women in my lives.

01:08:09.919 --> 01:08:11.253
I had to be better for my kids.

01:08:11.253 --> 01:08:12.688
I have to be better

01:08:12.688 --> 01:08:14.857
and they wanna be better.

01:08:14.857 --> 01:08:16.559
And that's the thing that I
always tell people on Queer

01:08:16.559 --> 01:08:18.527
and they're like,
what's the magic touch.

01:08:18.527 --> 01:08:20.029
Anybody can talk
about your trauma.

01:08:20.029 --> 01:08:22.498
The reason that these men
have these emotional moments

01:08:22.498 --> 01:08:26.335
with me is because I'm giving
them permission to know that

01:08:26.335 --> 01:08:30.339
their masculinity is not
invalidated by emotion.

01:08:30.339 --> 01:08:33.042
And I think it's so important
for men just to know that

01:08:33.042 --> 01:08:35.878
like, whatever you've
constructed of masculinity

01:08:35.878 --> 01:08:37.880
in your mind is
not gonna be broken

01:08:37.880 --> 01:08:40.716
just because you say
to someone I'm scared,

01:08:40.716 --> 01:08:43.319
because you say to
someone like I'm confused,

01:08:43.319 --> 01:08:46.622
it actually makes
you stronger, sexier.

01:08:46.622 --> 01:08:47.890
It makes you all these things

01:08:47.890 --> 01:08:49.492
that you actually
thinking you're not.

01:08:49.492 --> 01:08:51.894
Like I've never ran into
a woman who was like

01:08:51.894 --> 01:08:54.096
on dating this guy and
he was honest with me

01:08:54.096 --> 01:08:55.698
and it turned me off.

01:08:56.832 --> 01:08:58.234
That's not a thing guys.

01:08:58.234 --> 01:08:59.802
Like, you know what I mean?

01:08:59.802 --> 01:09:01.003
Or like, oh my gosh,

01:09:01.003 --> 01:09:02.638
we had a conversation
and he let me in,

01:09:02.638 --> 01:09:05.007
now I'm turned off.

01:09:05.007 --> 01:09:07.877
That's literally the exact
opposite that men should learn.

01:09:07.877 --> 01:09:10.279
And I think if we could
start telling them that,

01:09:10.279 --> 01:09:12.481
it even just gives them more
permission just to be open

01:09:12.481 --> 01:09:15.050
and honest about who they
are and what they're feeling.

01:09:15.050 --> 01:09:18.721
- Yeah, I think the real
emotional heart of the show

01:09:18.721 --> 01:09:23.025
is born out of
those moments where,

01:09:23.025 --> 01:09:26.462
you and your colleagues
are are showing,

01:09:26.462 --> 01:09:29.665
this person a
level of compassion

01:09:29.665 --> 01:09:32.401
that they've never experienced
in their life before.

01:09:32.401 --> 01:09:33.536
And when you sit down

01:09:33.536 --> 01:09:35.905
and give them permission
to be vulnerable

01:09:35.905 --> 01:09:38.274
or to ask them questions

01:09:38.274 --> 01:09:41.377
that nobody has ever asked
them before, like that's where,

01:09:41.377 --> 01:09:43.913
you're like, and you could
see the look on their face

01:09:43.913 --> 01:09:45.948
and the way that their
bodies feel is like,

01:09:45.948 --> 01:09:47.383
this is new to this.

01:09:47.383 --> 01:09:50.886
Like no one has ever shown
that kind of unconditional love

01:09:50.886 --> 01:09:53.222
and support for this person
in their entire life.

01:09:53.222 --> 01:09:54.056
- Yeah.

01:09:54.056 --> 01:09:55.491
And you know,

01:09:55.491 --> 01:09:57.860
for me it's so special
because I remember the day,

01:09:57.860 --> 01:09:59.328
I was probably like in,

01:09:59.328 --> 01:10:01.764
I was probably in ninth grade,
the minute that I realized

01:10:01.764 --> 01:10:04.600
that I needed to start
giving compassion to myself

01:10:04.600 --> 01:10:06.068
and to others in that way.

01:10:06.068 --> 01:10:10.606
And so it's interesting and
amazing that now I have a career

01:10:10.606 --> 01:10:13.008
where I do it publicly
and on television.

01:10:13.008 --> 01:10:14.777
But there's a story I wanna
share with you really quickly

01:10:14.777 --> 01:10:16.545
that I used to play
football in high school.

01:10:16.545 --> 01:10:18.614
And I remember we
would play football

01:10:18.614 --> 01:10:21.617
and while we were practicing
the girls would be doing their,

01:10:21.617 --> 01:10:23.152
running around the track.

01:10:23.152 --> 01:10:26.522
And I remember I got tackled
cause I was a wide receiver

01:10:26.522 --> 01:10:28.824
and my leg was busted up.

01:10:28.824 --> 01:10:30.159
I mean, like I was screwed up

01:10:30.159 --> 01:10:32.728
and all I remember is my
coach screaming at me,

01:10:32.728 --> 01:10:33.929
Brown, get up,

01:10:33.929 --> 01:10:35.331
Brown, run it all, Brown.

01:10:35.331 --> 01:10:36.532
And I'm sitting
here and I'm crying.

01:10:36.532 --> 01:10:37.800
He was like, Brown,
suck it up, go.

01:10:37.800 --> 01:10:38.901
Brown, get up.

01:10:38.901 --> 01:10:41.136
And I'm like in pain.

01:10:41.136 --> 01:10:43.172
And then I remember this girl

01:10:43.172 --> 01:10:44.406
and I'm not gonna say her name

01:10:44.406 --> 01:10:45.874
cause I'm still
friends with her but

01:10:47.343 --> 01:10:50.546
she was running a hurdle and
she tripped on the hurdle

01:10:50.546 --> 01:10:52.481
and she had the tiniest
scratch on her knee.

01:10:52.481 --> 01:10:55.684
And that same coach that was
just telling me to run off

01:10:55.684 --> 01:10:58.387
my then, I found
out my broken leg

01:10:58.387 --> 01:11:00.489
was like ran to her
with everyone else.

01:11:00.489 --> 01:11:01.890
And was like, oh my
gosh, what is wrong?

01:11:01.890 --> 01:11:02.691
What is going on?

01:11:02.691 --> 01:11:03.525
Are you okay?

01:11:03.525 --> 01:11:04.793
Okay, are you fine?

01:11:04.793 --> 01:11:06.595
And that was the day
I was like, F this.

01:11:06.595 --> 01:11:08.397
If you don't wanna give
me compassionate love,

01:11:08.397 --> 01:11:09.898
I got to give it to myself.

01:11:09.898 --> 01:11:11.567
And then I'm going to then
try to give to other people.

01:11:11.567 --> 01:11:15.304
And literally I quit and
became a peer counselor.

01:11:15.304 --> 01:11:16.805
I'm not even joking.

01:11:16.805 --> 01:11:20.209
His reaction set me on
a whole new path of like

01:11:20.209 --> 01:11:23.078
I'm now going to be a peer
counselor for other kids here.

01:11:23.078 --> 01:11:24.713
Also, it got me out of like,

01:11:24.713 --> 01:11:27.149
second and fifth period
because I was able to.

01:11:27.149 --> 01:11:28.951
So there was a little another
little motivation there.

01:11:28.951 --> 01:11:32.354
But I was like, if
you're gonna tell me

01:11:32.354 --> 01:11:36.358
that I have to endure pain
without any love or support,

01:11:36.358 --> 01:11:39.361
then I'm gonna make sure that
other people you tell that too

01:11:39.361 --> 01:11:41.997
can leave you and
come to me and,

01:11:41.997 --> 01:11:44.233
get the love and support
you're not giving them.

01:11:44.233 --> 01:11:46.635
And I literally started
doing that then,

01:11:46.635 --> 01:11:47.903
did it through college.

01:11:47.903 --> 01:11:50.806
And it's where I am now,
ironically my career.

01:11:50.806 --> 01:11:54.310
But I can pinpoint that moment
of him screaming at me Brown

01:11:55.811 --> 01:11:57.746
and this girl being
on the, you know.

01:11:57.746 --> 01:11:59.381
- Right, amazing, wow.

01:12:01.483 --> 01:12:04.386
It's gotta be hard to
practice that, like in

01:12:04.386 --> 01:12:06.422
quote unquote, all
your affairs, like,

01:12:08.123 --> 01:12:12.995
especially now where culture
just feels so fractured

01:12:12.995 --> 01:12:16.699
and I'm thinking back on
the particular episode

01:12:16.699 --> 01:12:20.903
of you guys with the cop,
which was, was that in.

01:12:20.903 --> 01:12:22.438
That was in the South
somewhere, I can't--

01:12:22.438 --> 01:12:24.707
- Yeah, we were in
Atlanta for that and,

01:12:24.707 --> 01:12:25.541
yeah, that was,

01:12:25.541 --> 01:12:26.909
a big one there.

01:12:26.909 --> 01:12:28.110
- We watched that.

01:12:28.110 --> 01:12:29.511
I think when we
watched that episode

01:12:29.511 --> 01:12:32.581
it was like right in the
vortex of George Floyd.

01:12:32.581 --> 01:12:35.718
So yeah, it was
like the timing of,

01:12:35.718 --> 01:12:38.654
watching that show, which was
obviously taped much earlier.

01:12:38.654 --> 01:12:43.659
It was so like challenging
to get on board with the idea

01:12:46.595 --> 01:12:48.464
that you guys are
gonna help this guy

01:12:48.464 --> 01:12:51.266
after you get pulled over
and that little stunt.

01:12:51.266 --> 01:12:52.735
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:12:53.669 --> 01:12:57.139
It was a difficult moment

01:12:57.139 --> 01:12:59.508
because we all felt
really triggered,

01:12:59.508 --> 01:13:01.410
especially myself,
Tan and Jonathan

01:13:01.410 --> 01:13:04.646
who had experienced police
brutality because of,

01:13:04.646 --> 01:13:07.383
for Tan, I, our
race or nationality.

01:13:07.383 --> 01:13:09.418
And for Jonathan,
because of the fact that,

01:13:09.418 --> 01:13:13.021
being non binary,
people would harass him

01:13:13.021 --> 01:13:16.592
for being a human
being who has a beard

01:13:16.592 --> 01:13:18.394
but also is in a skirt.

01:13:18.394 --> 01:13:21.830
And we understood like
real police brutality.

01:13:21.830 --> 01:13:23.732
We understood that
like there were police

01:13:23.732 --> 01:13:25.667
that are not here to
protect and serve you.

01:13:25.667 --> 01:13:27.870
There are some that are bad.

01:13:29.505 --> 01:13:31.206
And it, you know

01:13:31.206 --> 01:13:33.375
in that moment it was
like, okay, this is hard.

01:13:33.375 --> 01:13:36.211
But I think one of
my greatest gifts

01:13:36.211 --> 01:13:38.781
and I was talking
about this with Chris

01:13:38.781 --> 01:13:41.183
when I mentioned earlier
the other day is I was like

01:13:41.183 --> 01:13:44.820
I don't mind going to
spaces where people

01:13:44.820 --> 01:13:49.024
who feel differently than me
and trying to make a bridge.

01:13:49.024 --> 01:13:51.126
And so I saw that as an
opportunity with this cop

01:13:51.126 --> 01:13:53.595
of being like, hey
this is a moment for us

01:13:53.595 --> 01:13:55.197
to see each other.

01:13:55.197 --> 01:13:57.833
And it doesn't mean that it's
gonna change how you police.

01:13:57.833 --> 01:14:00.402
It doesn't mean it's gonna
change how your friends police,

01:14:00.402 --> 01:14:03.038
but hopefully if I tell
you that my son at 16,

01:14:03.038 --> 01:14:04.473
didn't wanna get his license,

01:14:04.473 --> 01:14:06.208
something that we all wanna do.

01:14:06.208 --> 01:14:09.011
If I tell you this, maybe
I can get to your heart.

01:14:09.011 --> 01:14:11.880
And maybe I can, instead of
trying to change your mind,

01:14:11.880 --> 01:14:13.348
I can change your heart.

01:14:13.348 --> 01:14:16.485
And then the next kid you
see that may look like my son

01:14:16.485 --> 01:14:18.554
you'll stop and think,
is this Karamo's son

01:14:18.554 --> 01:14:20.055
before you make your choice.

01:14:21.123 --> 01:14:23.258
And, it was powerful.

01:14:23.258 --> 01:14:24.593
Even in the episode

01:14:24.593 --> 01:14:27.196
you could see he's
physically bawling at the end

01:14:27.196 --> 01:14:30.632
cause he's like, our car ride
just meant the most to him

01:14:30.632 --> 01:14:33.168
because he was met with
empathy and saying like,

01:14:33.168 --> 01:14:37.105
hey, were at the end of the
day we want the same things.

01:14:37.105 --> 01:14:39.408
We've just had these
experiences that put us

01:14:39.408 --> 01:14:40.609
on a different path.

01:14:40.609 --> 01:14:41.910
But if you go back
to your heart,

01:14:41.910 --> 01:14:43.512
you go back to your emotions,

01:14:43.512 --> 01:14:46.348
you can get back on
that path of love and,

01:14:46.348 --> 01:14:47.182
all those things.

01:14:47.182 --> 01:14:48.317
And so,

01:14:48.317 --> 01:14:49.251
it's really nice.

01:14:49.251 --> 01:14:50.919
- I mean, it's so powerful.

01:14:50.919 --> 01:14:54.623
And I look at that as
almost like a template or,

01:14:54.623 --> 01:14:57.292
an instruction manual
for how to interface

01:14:57.292 --> 01:14:58.260
with the world right now.

01:14:58.260 --> 01:15:00.362
We're on the back end of like

01:15:00.362 --> 01:15:04.199
this unbelievably
divisive election,

01:15:04.199 --> 01:15:06.268
I don't know in my lifetime,

01:15:06.268 --> 01:15:09.304
a period in which
we were so divided.

01:15:09.304 --> 01:15:13.475
This unbelievable breakdown
in our ability to communicate

01:15:13.475 --> 01:15:16.645
in a healthy way and
identify common ground,

01:15:16.645 --> 01:15:18.881
when in truth we
share so much more

01:15:18.881 --> 01:15:21.149
than that which divides us.

01:15:21.149 --> 01:15:23.385
And I spent a lot of
time thinking about,

01:15:23.385 --> 01:15:27.523
as a public facing person,
like, how do I talk about this?

01:15:27.523 --> 01:15:30.459
How do I interface with
people who disagree with me?

01:15:30.459 --> 01:15:33.228
And I'm very much somebody
who doesn't court controversy,

01:15:33.228 --> 01:15:34.663
I wanna build bridges.

01:15:34.663 --> 01:15:37.032
But I also feel
there are moments

01:15:37.032 --> 01:15:39.835
where it's important to
speak your truth even though,

01:15:39.835 --> 01:15:41.436
you know you're
gonna get attacked

01:15:41.436 --> 01:15:43.539
or whatever's gonna
happen with that.

01:15:43.539 --> 01:15:48.544
And everything is on such
a tight trigger right now.

01:15:49.611 --> 01:15:51.146
It's like, everybody's
ready to snap.

01:15:51.146 --> 01:15:54.449
And I feel like the only
way forward for our culture

01:15:54.449 --> 01:15:56.618
if we wanna have
a shot at being,

01:15:56.618 --> 01:15:58.954
the United States of America,

01:15:58.954 --> 01:16:01.290
is figuring out how to build
those individual bridges

01:16:01.290 --> 01:16:02.724
in our personal lives.

01:16:02.724 --> 01:16:03.792
How do we communicate?

01:16:03.792 --> 01:16:05.961
Like, okay, Thanksgiving
just happen.

01:16:05.961 --> 01:16:07.829
We're coming up on,

01:16:07.829 --> 01:16:09.865
the bigger holidays.

01:16:09.865 --> 01:16:11.600
Like how do you
interact with people

01:16:11.600 --> 01:16:13.302
who see the world
differently from you?

01:16:13.302 --> 01:16:14.903
How can you find
that common ground?

01:16:14.903 --> 01:16:16.138
And how can,

01:16:16.138 --> 01:16:20.976
we really embrace the
things that we share

01:16:20.976 --> 01:16:25.614
and transcend all
of the divisiveness

01:16:25.614 --> 01:16:27.082
that's really dragging us down

01:16:27.082 --> 01:16:30.552
and making us anxious
and depressed.

01:16:30.552 --> 01:16:32.020
And it's really,

01:16:32.020 --> 01:16:34.489
it's deeply concerning
to me when I look at

01:16:34.489 --> 01:16:35.757
what's happening out there.

01:16:35.757 --> 01:16:37.292
- Well, the first thing
I say to people is that

01:16:37.292 --> 01:16:38.827
we need to get, as a society,

01:16:38.827 --> 01:16:40.596
we need to get rid
of cancel culture

01:16:40.596 --> 01:16:42.464
and switch it with
council culture.

01:16:42.464 --> 01:16:45.834
I think that we have
gotten to a place where,

01:16:45.834 --> 01:16:48.804
it's easy to jump on a
bandwagon, council someone.

01:16:48.804 --> 01:16:51.707
But then what we forget is
that once you've council them

01:16:51.707 --> 01:16:54.910
all they do is go, they get
further pushed into a group

01:16:54.910 --> 01:16:57.746
of people who believe and
feel the same way they feel.

01:16:57.746 --> 01:17:00.649
It's very rare as we've
seen in these past couple

01:17:00.649 --> 01:17:02.851
of years where cancel
culture, they're popular.

01:17:02.851 --> 01:17:04.453
It's very rare where someone

01:17:04.453 --> 01:17:06.922
who's been canceled
actually changes

01:17:06.922 --> 01:17:08.790
either privately or publicly.

01:17:08.790 --> 01:17:11.159
All do is they revert
back to a group

01:17:11.159 --> 01:17:13.095
that believes what they believe.

01:17:13.095 --> 01:17:15.230
And I think that's
part of the problem.

01:17:15.230 --> 01:17:16.665
Instead of canceling them,

01:17:16.665 --> 01:17:17.866
we should be giving these
people opportunities

01:17:17.866 --> 01:17:20.836
to sit down and be
counseled by someone.

01:17:20.836 --> 01:17:23.205
And I think council culture
is a very healthier way

01:17:23.205 --> 01:17:25.807
of saying your
actions were wrong.

01:17:25.807 --> 01:17:28.577
Your actions were wrong
because this is how it made

01:17:28.577 --> 01:17:30.078
this group of people feel.

01:17:30.078 --> 01:17:33.649
Now, let's talk about why
did you feel it was okay

01:17:33.649 --> 01:17:35.150
to hurt this group of people?

01:17:35.150 --> 01:17:36.852
Why did you feel it was
okay to hurt this person?

01:17:36.852 --> 01:17:40.889
What in your own past led you
to think that this was okay?

01:17:40.889 --> 01:17:44.259
What part of your power access
makes you think that this is

01:17:44.259 --> 01:17:46.128
the appropriate way to react?

01:17:46.128 --> 01:17:48.530
Giving people
opportunities to grow

01:17:48.530 --> 01:17:49.765
and learn instead
of canceling them

01:17:49.765 --> 01:17:51.266
is I think so important.

01:17:51.266 --> 01:17:54.770
But to do that, you have
to have a group of people

01:17:54.770 --> 01:17:57.572
who understand the importance
of empathetic listening.

01:17:57.572 --> 01:17:59.174
So when I go into
situations with people

01:17:59.174 --> 01:18:00.642
who are different than me,

01:18:00.642 --> 01:18:02.244
the first thing I think about
is some of my grandma used

01:18:02.244 --> 01:18:03.645
to say, immigrants
used to say which is

01:18:03.645 --> 01:18:05.147
you have two of these,
you have one of these.

01:18:05.147 --> 01:18:06.648
Two ears, one mouth.

01:18:06.648 --> 01:18:08.684
So you're supposed to be
doing one double time and

01:18:08.684 --> 01:18:10.686
one a little less.

01:18:10.686 --> 01:18:12.854
And I think about
that all the time.

01:18:12.854 --> 01:18:15.390
And so when I go into something,
instead of me jumping into

01:18:15.390 --> 01:18:18.360
a conversation and like, just
trying to defend my point

01:18:18.360 --> 01:18:20.295
or like tell you why
you're wrong or whatever

01:18:20.295 --> 01:18:21.596
because I now know as an adult,

01:18:21.596 --> 01:18:23.231
that's gonna only
shut them down.

01:18:23.231 --> 01:18:25.200
I go into and I say, hey,

01:18:25.200 --> 01:18:29.204
let's start the premise
with your actions hurt

01:18:29.204 --> 01:18:31.873
this person, me, someone else.

01:18:31.873 --> 01:18:35.477
I wanna hear why you
made that action.

01:18:35.477 --> 01:18:37.245
And then I don't
go into anything.

01:18:37.245 --> 01:18:38.280
And this is key.

01:18:38.280 --> 01:18:39.414
I'm now listening.

01:18:39.414 --> 01:18:41.049
But within the listening

01:18:41.049 --> 01:18:42.651
something else that
I think people do

01:18:42.651 --> 01:18:46.254
is they then start to
conjure up ways to defend

01:18:46.254 --> 01:18:48.757
a point of view that
they don't even know yet.

01:18:48.757 --> 01:18:50.726
So you assume the other
person is gonna say something.

01:18:50.726 --> 01:18:52.160
So you already have your,

01:18:53.562 --> 01:18:54.696
what you're gonna say back.

01:18:54.696 --> 01:18:55.931
It happens in
couples all the time.

01:18:55.931 --> 01:18:57.132
I see it all the time.

01:18:57.132 --> 01:18:58.633
Every time I see a
breakdown in a couple

01:18:58.633 --> 01:19:00.335
it's because they're already
ready, they're defensive.

01:19:00.335 --> 01:19:01.470
It's like, I know what
I'm gonna say to you

01:19:01.470 --> 01:19:03.171
to get you back to say it back.

01:19:03.171 --> 01:19:04.639
And we have to release that.

01:19:04.639 --> 01:19:06.074
So if I'm gonna listen to you,

01:19:06.074 --> 01:19:08.376
I'm gonna listen to you without
having a rebuttal already.

01:19:08.376 --> 01:19:09.878
And I think that's so key.

01:19:09.878 --> 01:19:11.947
So instead of canceling
you, I'm gonna counsel you

01:19:11.947 --> 01:19:14.049
by listening and not
having a rebuttal.

01:19:14.049 --> 01:19:18.386
And I think you have to
then also understand if

01:19:18.386 --> 01:19:21.156
is this person in a space
to actually receive this?

01:19:21.156 --> 01:19:23.358
Because I think sometimes
we get blue in the face.

01:19:23.358 --> 01:19:26.361
We're trying to talk to
people who are just not ready.

01:19:26.361 --> 01:19:27.829
They're just not ready.

01:19:27.829 --> 01:19:30.599
And also they're not ready
because they've been triggered

01:19:30.599 --> 01:19:33.168
by the last 10 peoples
who try to talk to them,

01:19:33.168 --> 01:19:35.704
who didn't do it properly,
who all told they were wrong.

01:19:35.704 --> 01:19:37.172
And so you have to say,

01:19:37.172 --> 01:19:40.175
well, am I really in a
space to wanna build trust

01:19:40.175 --> 01:19:43.845
and build a bigger connection?

01:19:43.845 --> 01:19:47.616
Like in my career so far, I've
been canceled probably a lot

01:19:47.616 --> 01:19:50.252
but I get counseled often
because I do believe.

01:19:50.252 --> 01:19:53.355
Yeah, I get counseled
all the time.

01:19:53.355 --> 01:19:55.724
I'll give you some
examples of recent.

01:19:55.724 --> 01:19:59.761
I get canceled
because people say,

01:19:59.761 --> 01:20:01.496
why would you talk
to this person?

01:20:01.496 --> 01:20:03.431
Why would you interact
with this person?

01:20:03.431 --> 01:20:05.000
But it goes to what we
talked about at the beginning

01:20:05.000 --> 01:20:06.902
of this interview is,

01:20:06.902 --> 01:20:10.338
I didn't have the luxury being
black gay immigrant parents

01:20:10.338 --> 01:20:13.141
to not try to reach out to those
who were different from me.

01:20:13.141 --> 01:20:14.176
I didn't have that luxury know.

01:20:14.176 --> 01:20:15.677
I never had that luxury.

01:20:15.677 --> 01:20:17.646
The only way I've succeeded

01:20:17.646 --> 01:20:21.449
and find a healthy
boundary is by saying,

01:20:21.449 --> 01:20:23.685
I got to meet you
where you're at

01:20:23.685 --> 01:20:25.654
because that person was
never gonna meet me.

01:20:25.654 --> 01:20:27.322
And so it's always
been my burden

01:20:27.322 --> 01:20:29.124
but always has been
my greatest triumph

01:20:29.124 --> 01:20:32.294
of being able to say, I know I
can meet you where you're at.

01:20:32.294 --> 01:20:34.029
And I think,

01:20:34.029 --> 01:20:39.034
like I got canceled last year
because I called Sean Spicer,

01:20:40.235 --> 01:20:41.503
Trump's former press
secretary, a nice guy,

01:20:41.503 --> 01:20:43.071
and then tried to
engage with him.

01:20:43.071 --> 01:20:44.439
- All right, cause you guys
did Dancing with the Stars.

01:20:44.439 --> 01:20:46.675
- Did Dancing with the
Stars together and he,

01:20:46.675 --> 01:20:48.743
I don't agree with
any of his politics

01:20:48.743 --> 01:20:50.478
but I was like, you know what?

01:20:50.478 --> 01:20:53.481
If there's a way that I
can maybe help this man

01:20:53.481 --> 01:20:56.384
to see how his actions
are effecting people,

01:20:56.384 --> 01:20:58.286
I love and affecting my life.

01:20:58.286 --> 01:20:59.688
Maybe he will,

01:20:59.688 --> 01:21:01.556
his heart will
change just enough

01:21:01.556 --> 01:21:03.491
that when he's
around some people

01:21:03.491 --> 01:21:05.026
who would never be around me,

01:21:05.026 --> 01:21:07.696
he can like, it will start to
come out of him unconsciously,

01:21:07.696 --> 01:21:09.431
where he's starting
to talk like Karamo.

01:21:09.431 --> 01:21:10.665
He doesn't even realize it

01:21:10.665 --> 01:21:12.934
because it's like
I've hit his heart.

01:21:12.934 --> 01:21:15.670
And people were like, I can't
believe you said he was nice.

01:21:15.670 --> 01:21:17.706
And I was like, I didn't
say his politics were nice.

01:21:17.706 --> 01:21:21.076
I said, him as a human being,
I could see him as a human.

01:21:22.110 --> 01:21:23.111
I could see his fears.

01:21:23.111 --> 01:21:24.446
I could see his pain.

01:21:24.446 --> 01:21:26.414
I could see the manipulation.

01:21:26.414 --> 01:21:27.449
I could see everything in him.

01:21:27.449 --> 01:21:29.618
And I could remove that and say,

01:21:29.618 --> 01:21:32.621
well, now let me try to
help you to even be better.

01:21:32.621 --> 01:21:33.889
I went visited--

01:21:33.889 --> 01:21:35.390
- You have to be able
to be able to do that.

01:21:35.390 --> 01:21:38.460
If the solution is you just
never have anything to do

01:21:38.460 --> 01:21:40.328
with that person because
they don't see the world

01:21:40.328 --> 01:21:41.730
the way that you
do, we're doomed

01:21:41.730 --> 01:21:42.964
- We're doomed.

01:21:42.964 --> 01:21:44.232
And that's where we
are as a culture.

01:21:44.232 --> 01:21:45.867
We are, and that's why I say,

01:21:45.867 --> 01:21:48.770
get rid of cancel culture,
put it with council culture,

01:21:48.770 --> 01:21:50.472
be more empathetic at listening.

01:21:51.706 --> 01:21:54.976
Really be there and not
to like have a rebuttal

01:21:54.976 --> 01:21:57.145
and just really understand
if the person is ready.

01:21:57.145 --> 01:21:59.447
And I think then we'll
start to get to a place

01:21:59.447 --> 01:22:01.483
where more people are
open, honest, vulnerable

01:22:01.483 --> 01:22:04.519
and finding common ground.

01:22:04.519 --> 01:22:06.621
Because I wanna end on this.

01:22:06.621 --> 01:22:08.790
If the aliens came down tomorrow

01:22:08.790 --> 01:22:10.292
not saying I believe in aliens,

01:22:10.292 --> 01:22:13.428
but if the aliens came down
tomorrow, I promise you

01:22:13.428 --> 01:22:15.764
none of us are gonna
be like you're black,

01:22:15.764 --> 01:22:17.365
you're white, you're
gay, you're straight.

01:22:17.365 --> 01:22:18.900
We're gonna be like, oh, shit.

01:22:18.900 --> 01:22:20.769
It's us against these aliens
who are about to eat us all.

01:22:20.769 --> 01:22:22.804
And so, not calling the aliens,

01:22:22.804 --> 01:22:24.439
if there are like, they're mean,

01:22:24.439 --> 01:22:25.674
but I'm just saying like

01:22:25.674 --> 01:22:28.076
we have to come back to
a place like right now

01:22:28.076 --> 01:22:29.911
we've made each other the enemy.

01:22:29.911 --> 01:22:31.346
And like, at the end of the day,

01:22:31.346 --> 01:22:32.881
we're all trying to
survive on this big planet

01:22:32.881 --> 01:22:35.216
where things are alive and
can kill us all at any moment.

01:22:35.216 --> 01:22:37.319
So let's all just come
together and realize that.

01:22:37.319 --> 01:22:39.587
- Yeah, here's the
pessimistic note though.

01:22:39.587 --> 01:22:44.125
You would think replace
aliens with pandemic.

01:22:44.125 --> 01:22:46.528
Like if a pandemic happened,
we would all have to unite

01:22:46.528 --> 01:22:49.731
and collectively figure out
how to solve this problem.

01:22:49.731 --> 01:22:51.433
And we all know what
we're seeing right now

01:22:51.433 --> 01:22:56.438
which is an exacerbation of
this divide in a way that

01:22:57.639 --> 01:22:58.873
I don't know if any of
us could have predicted.

01:22:58.873 --> 01:23:00.408
But I think your
point is well taken.

01:23:00.408 --> 01:23:01.609
And it really is--

01:23:01.609 --> 01:23:03.144
- But even when this
pandemic, I'll say this,

01:23:03.144 --> 01:23:05.613
I think the problem was
not just the (indistinct).

01:23:06.781 --> 01:23:08.717
- Politicized, I can't.

01:23:08.717 --> 01:23:09.818
- Politicization.

01:23:09.818 --> 01:23:11.019
- You cancel.

01:23:11.019 --> 01:23:12.654
- Exactly, cancel.

01:23:12.654 --> 01:23:14.656
It's not just because
they politicize that,

01:23:14.656 --> 01:23:16.624
with the mask and
everything else.

01:23:16.624 --> 01:23:19.461
I think it's also because again,

01:23:19.461 --> 01:23:22.330
when you don't touch
someone's emotions

01:23:22.330 --> 01:23:24.666
then their mind
will never change.

01:23:24.666 --> 01:23:27.902
And so I don't want anyone
else to die from this pandemic.

01:23:27.902 --> 01:23:29.337
So let me be very clear.

01:23:29.337 --> 01:23:32.874
But I think the fact
that the number of deaths

01:23:32.874 --> 01:23:36.711
weren't affecting so
many people's homes,
that they really,

01:23:36.711 --> 01:23:39.180
a lot of people weren't
seeing their grandmas pass.

01:23:39.180 --> 01:23:40.915
People were getting,

01:23:40.915 --> 01:23:42.417
recovering really quickly.

01:23:42.417 --> 01:23:45.453
If this pandemic would
have really unfortunately

01:23:45.453 --> 01:23:46.654
taken more lives.

01:23:46.654 --> 01:23:47.856
And I'm not saying
that the lives

01:23:47.856 --> 01:23:50.358
that were taken
were undervalued but

01:23:50.358 --> 01:23:52.861
I think then we would have
seen the common enemy,

01:23:52.861 --> 01:23:54.896
and it didn't matter how
they try to politicize it.

01:23:54.896 --> 01:23:56.731
It would've been like,
no, we're all in this.

01:23:56.731 --> 01:23:59.000
And I think that's
what we went wrong

01:23:59.000 --> 01:24:00.902
when we were fighting
people with mask.

01:24:00.902 --> 01:24:03.872
I never tried to have like
the mask conversation early on

01:24:03.872 --> 01:24:05.507
because I was like,
I'm not gonna get you.

01:24:05.507 --> 01:24:07.442
If you've already been told
them, (indistinct) stupid,

01:24:07.442 --> 01:24:09.744
your mind is set.

01:24:09.744 --> 01:24:14.749
But if I can show you how
my sister or my cousin

01:24:15.617 --> 01:24:17.986
or my aunt is sick right now

01:24:17.986 --> 01:24:19.954
and really get to you one-on-one

01:24:19.954 --> 01:24:22.023
not just on a Facebook
feed, just one-on-one,

01:24:22.023 --> 01:24:24.192
talk to somebody, that's
where it got better.

01:24:24.192 --> 01:24:26.594
And I spent a lot of time in
the early days of pandemic

01:24:26.594 --> 01:24:29.164
and doing Zoom calls
with people who did not,

01:24:29.164 --> 01:24:31.599
all my podcasts, which I
can never use any of it

01:24:31.599 --> 01:24:32.734
because when my
podcast comes back out,

01:24:32.734 --> 01:24:35.203
like it's gonna be gone.

01:24:35.203 --> 01:24:36.037
But it was like.

01:24:36.037 --> 01:24:37.705
- Everything changes.

01:24:37.705 --> 01:24:39.307
- You see differently,

01:24:39.307 --> 01:24:40.742
can I just talk to you?

01:24:40.742 --> 01:24:42.243
And through that conversation,

01:24:42.243 --> 01:24:45.113
I would say about eight out of
10 will change their hearts.

01:24:45.113 --> 01:24:47.015
And I saw it physically
on their Instagram

01:24:47.015 --> 01:24:48.650
where they will all
of a sudden be like,

01:24:48.650 --> 01:24:51.586
damn, I didn't know that this
was really affecting someone.

01:24:51.586 --> 01:24:54.389
So that's to play
on your pessimists.

01:24:54.389 --> 01:24:58.893
- Well, to be sure things like
podcasts are super important

01:24:58.893 --> 01:25:01.696
because you've got to be
able to create the space

01:25:01.696 --> 01:25:04.933
and the bandwidth and
allow for the nuance

01:25:04.933 --> 01:25:06.101
and the back and forth.

01:25:06.101 --> 01:25:08.103
Like this is not
happening on Twitter.

01:25:08.103 --> 01:25:12.640
And if we want a
shot at bridging this
divide, we've gotta,

01:25:12.640 --> 01:25:16.244
divorce ourselves a little
bit from those platforms

01:25:16.244 --> 01:25:19.013
and invest a little bit more
on that one-on-one amen.

01:25:19.013 --> 01:25:19.948
- Amen.

01:25:19.948 --> 01:25:21.683
And invest in the one-on-one.

01:25:22.884 --> 01:25:24.352
- We'll round this
out in a minute,

01:25:24.352 --> 01:25:27.021
but I can't let you go without
asking you one more thing,

01:25:27.021 --> 01:25:29.290
which is to...

01:25:29.290 --> 01:25:31.159
- No, you don't need
to be on Queer Eye.

01:25:31.159 --> 01:25:34.596
(indistinct), right now
that you do not need to be,

01:25:34.596 --> 01:25:37.499
you're literally the after photo
of what we do on Queer Eye.

01:25:37.499 --> 01:25:38.733
I'm just gonna let you
know that right now,

01:25:38.733 --> 01:25:40.068
you're not coming on the show.

01:25:40.068 --> 01:25:41.736
- Oh, my goal is achieved.

01:25:42.637 --> 01:25:43.571
Thank you.

01:25:44.539 --> 01:25:46.141
I'm interested in your thoughts

01:25:46.141 --> 01:25:50.578
on your experience of
working with so many people,

01:25:50.578 --> 01:25:54.549
what are the common things
that tend to trip people up

01:25:54.549 --> 01:25:57.318
and kind of show
up his blind spots?

01:25:57.318 --> 01:26:01.122
Like, what's the thing that
when you look around you see

01:26:02.290 --> 01:26:03.925
that guy just knew
this one thing,

01:26:03.925 --> 01:26:07.295
like he could transform himself

01:26:07.295 --> 01:26:08.830
or herself.
- Communication.

01:26:08.830 --> 01:26:13.668
Communication, it's funny as
a species who uses language

01:26:15.103 --> 01:26:16.905
whether it's through your
voice, through sign language

01:26:16.905 --> 01:26:21.809
to interact with others, we are
such horrible communicators.

01:26:21.809 --> 01:26:24.946
We don't communicate to
ourselves what we're feeling.

01:26:24.946 --> 01:26:27.515
We don't communicate to
others what we're feeling,

01:26:27.515 --> 01:26:29.450
our communication is so broken.

01:26:29.450 --> 01:26:33.621
We get into conversations
and we stop ourselves.

01:26:33.621 --> 01:26:35.523
Like we talked about
earlier in the conversation

01:26:35.523 --> 01:26:36.357
where you just,

01:26:37.592 --> 01:26:39.294
you close off your voice.

01:26:39.294 --> 01:26:42.697
You stop speaking,
you stop sharing.

01:26:43.831 --> 01:26:45.200
And I think that's the thing

01:26:45.200 --> 01:26:46.701
that I would just
want most people,

01:26:46.701 --> 01:26:48.169
every person I come across with.

01:26:48.169 --> 01:26:49.637
I'm like, well,

01:26:49.637 --> 01:26:51.206
why didn't you ever tell
anybody this was happening?

01:26:51.206 --> 01:26:52.440
Why didn't you ever admit

01:26:52.440 --> 01:26:54.776
to yourself that
this was going on?

01:26:54.776 --> 01:26:56.244
Why didn't you ever share?

01:26:56.244 --> 01:26:58.947
And it's like, I didn't know
how to communicate this.

01:26:58.947 --> 01:27:01.616
- And asking for help
is weakness or scary.

01:27:01.616 --> 01:27:03.117
- Asking for help is weakness.

01:27:03.117 --> 01:27:04.819
But again, asking for help
as a form of communication.

01:27:04.819 --> 01:27:08.523
And so it all kinda for me,
goes back to communication.

01:27:08.523 --> 01:27:10.391
And I think if we
could really give,

01:27:11.759 --> 01:27:15.430
not to go back, but if we
instead in school taught people

01:27:15.430 --> 01:27:17.031
who were never gonna use,

01:27:18.199 --> 01:27:20.635
trigonometry ever
again in their lives

01:27:20.635 --> 01:27:23.605
a class that would teach
them how to communicate.

01:27:23.605 --> 01:27:26.341
I loved English and I
loved reading books.

01:27:26.341 --> 01:27:27.942
It was one of my
favorite subjects,

01:27:27.942 --> 01:27:31.613
but no one in that class taught
me how to truly communicate

01:27:31.613 --> 01:27:32.814
what's going on.

01:27:32.814 --> 01:27:34.182
That's not a skill
we're really learning.

01:27:34.182 --> 01:27:36.651
You learn it from the
streets, your home.

01:27:36.651 --> 01:27:39.220
And that's where
we get caught up.

01:27:39.220 --> 01:27:40.755
I see it on Queer all the time.

01:27:40.755 --> 01:27:42.123
I literally like,

01:27:42.123 --> 01:27:43.992
why didn't you communicate
this to anybody?

01:27:43.992 --> 01:27:45.493
Why didn't you communicate
this to yourself?

01:27:45.493 --> 01:27:46.527
Why didn't you ask for help?

01:27:46.527 --> 01:27:47.996
Why what was going on?

01:27:47.996 --> 01:27:50.598
And that fear
around communication

01:27:50.598 --> 01:27:53.234
is the biggest issue
that stops us all.

01:27:53.234 --> 01:27:55.637
- Yeah, and then the
release that you see

01:27:55.637 --> 01:27:57.272
when they finally
are going to do it,

01:27:57.272 --> 01:27:59.807
it's like they suddenly
lose 20 pounds.

01:27:59.807 --> 01:28:01.209
- Completely.

01:28:01.209 --> 01:28:03.177
So I would encourage
people in your friendships,

01:28:03.177 --> 01:28:04.979
in your relationships,

01:28:04.979 --> 01:28:09.584
as parents, highlight
communication more,

01:28:09.584 --> 01:28:12.353
encourage your kids, encourage
your friends to talk more,

01:28:12.353 --> 01:28:14.555
be patient with them
as they're talking,

01:28:14.555 --> 01:28:15.923
which I think is so important.

01:28:15.923 --> 01:28:17.925
Like, people don't always,

01:28:17.925 --> 01:28:20.461
not all of us or
(indistinct) we can just,

01:28:20.461 --> 01:28:22.363
share a speech
and say something.

01:28:22.363 --> 01:28:25.466
Sometimes things come out
confusing, it comes out wrong.

01:28:25.466 --> 01:28:27.702
This is something that I've
been dealing with right now,

01:28:27.702 --> 01:28:30.104
with someone of like
being patient with them

01:28:30.104 --> 01:28:32.373
and understanding like your
intention is not to hurt me.

01:28:32.373 --> 01:28:35.376
You're just learning how to
communicate in a new way.

01:28:35.376 --> 01:28:38.146
And I think it's so
important because

01:28:38.146 --> 01:28:39.814
when you can let
someone communicate,

01:28:39.814 --> 01:28:41.449
when we can
communicate together,

01:28:41.449 --> 01:28:45.620
when you communicate to yourself
honestly, world opened up.

01:28:45.620 --> 01:28:48.556
- That's a beautiful
way to end it my friend.

01:28:48.556 --> 01:28:51.125
You're beautiful man, Karamo.

01:28:51.125 --> 01:28:52.360
You're an inspiration.

01:28:52.360 --> 01:28:54.095
I wish you only well.
- So are you my friend,

01:28:54.095 --> 01:28:55.363
I'm so glade to be here.

01:28:55.363 --> 01:28:57.999
As a fan of your show,
I'm so happy to on it.

01:28:57.999 --> 01:28:59.000
- The honor is all mine.

01:28:59.000 --> 01:29:00.601
When is the show resume?

01:29:00.601 --> 01:29:03.104
Is it you guys have a production
schedule, what's going on?

01:29:03.104 --> 01:29:05.039
- No, they haven't told
us probably February

01:29:05.039 --> 01:29:06.274
because we're,

01:29:06.274 --> 01:29:07.842
our show is all about
connection, hugging.

01:29:07.842 --> 01:29:09.110
The minute that goes
out for the show,

01:29:09.110 --> 01:29:10.278
you've missed it.
- You can't,

01:29:10.278 --> 01:29:11.112
what are you gonna do?

01:29:11.112 --> 01:29:11.946
- Yeah, you can't.

01:29:11.946 --> 01:29:12.780
- There is no show.

01:29:12.780 --> 01:29:13.715
- There's no show.

01:29:13.715 --> 01:29:14.515
We can't be six feet apart.

01:29:14.515 --> 01:29:15.917
You know what I mean.

01:29:15.917 --> 01:29:17.352
So we're trying to
figure that out now.

01:29:17.352 --> 01:29:20.822
And so for me, I don't get
political but I'm very thankful

01:29:20.822 --> 01:29:22.223
that we have a president now

01:29:22.223 --> 01:29:23.658
who's gonna take the
pandemic serious.

01:29:23.658 --> 01:29:25.326
And I'm also,

01:29:25.326 --> 01:29:26.561
I wanna highlight the fact

01:29:26.561 --> 01:29:29.263
which I think has gotten
washed under too much.

01:29:29.263 --> 01:29:32.467
The fact that we have our
first female vice president,

01:29:32.467 --> 01:29:36.270
who I just, I'm so glad
that we now have a woman

01:29:36.270 --> 01:29:37.205
in that office.

01:29:37.205 --> 01:29:38.473
And I hope that,

01:29:38.473 --> 01:29:41.743
and I know that she
will not be the last

01:29:41.743 --> 01:29:44.045
and I can't wait for the
day that both the president

01:29:44.045 --> 01:29:46.714
vice president and speaker
of the house are all women.

01:29:46.714 --> 01:29:49.517
And, I promise you, our
country is gonna be great.

01:29:49.517 --> 01:29:51.152
We already know what
happens when men run it.

01:29:51.152 --> 01:29:52.253
It's shitty.

01:29:52.253 --> 01:29:53.187
We've seen it.

01:29:53.187 --> 01:29:53.921
I'm just being real with you.

01:29:53.921 --> 01:29:54.756
We've seen it.

01:29:54.756 --> 01:29:55.790
Wars, everything.

01:29:55.790 --> 01:29:57.258
We started them.

01:29:57.258 --> 01:29:59.394
We did all this shit, famine,
all the we talking about.

01:29:59.394 --> 01:30:00.328
We did it.

01:30:00.328 --> 01:30:01.863
Women didn't do it, we did it.

01:30:01.863 --> 01:30:05.032
So let's put them in charge
and this is the first step.

01:30:05.032 --> 01:30:06.401
And I feel very optimistic.

01:30:08.803 --> 01:30:10.004
- Beautiful.

01:30:10.004 --> 01:30:11.272
Come back and talk
to me again sometime.

01:30:11.272 --> 01:30:12.774
- Anytime, you tell
me, we're neighbors.

01:30:12.774 --> 01:30:14.609
- (indistinct) peace, bless.

01:30:14.609 --> 01:30:17.211
(upbeat music)

